window.CILIN_QUESTIONS={"reading":[{"id":"reading-perkin-1","level":"cet4","title":"Perkin and the First Synthetic Dye","source":{"textId":"u1a","unit":"u1","label":"传记 Unit 1 · Text A · William Henry Perkin"},"passage":["William Henry Perkin was born on March 12, 1838, in London, England. As a boy, Perkin’s curiosity prompted early interests in the arts, sciences, photography, and engineering. But it was a chance stumbling upon a run-down, yet functional, laboratory in his late grandfather’s home that solidified the young man’s enthusiasm for chemistry. As a student at the City of London School, Perkin became immersed in the study of chemistry. His talent and devotion to the subject were perceived by his teacher, Thomas Hall, who encouraged him to attend a series of lectures given by the eminent scientist Michael Faraday at the Royal Institution. Perkin’s scientific gifts soon caught Hofmann’s attention and, within two years, he became Hofmann’s youngest assistant.","Not long after that, Perkin made the scientific breakthrough that would bring him both fame and fortune. At the time, quinine was the only viable medical treatment for malaria. The drug is derived from the bark of the cinchona tree, native to South America, and by 1856 demand for the drug was surpassing the available supply. He was attempting to manufacture quinine from aniline, an inexpensive and readily available coal tar waste product. Despite his best efforts, however, he did not end up with quinine. Instead, he produced a mysterious dark sludge.","Luckily, Perkin’s scientific training and nature prompted him to investigate the substance further. Incorporating potassium dichromate and alcohol into the aniline at various stages of the experimental process, he finally produced a deep purple solution. And, proving the truth of the famous scientist Louis Pasteur’s words ‘chance favours only the prepared mind’, Perkin saw the potential of his unexpected find. Historically, textile dyes were made from such natural sources as plants and animal excretions. Some of these, such as the glandular mucus of snails, were difficult to obtain and outrageously expensive. Indeed, the purple colour extracted from a snail was once so costly that in society at the time only the rich could afford it. Further, natural dyes tended to be muddy in hue and fade quickly. It was against this backdrop that Perkin’s discovery was made. Perkin quickly grasped that his purple solution could be used to colour fabric, thus making it the world’s first synthetic dye."],"items":[{"no":1,"stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A. How William Perkin discovered and developed the first synthetic dye.","B. Why quinine was in great demand in South America.","C. How natural dyes were extracted from plants and animals.","D. Why Perkin gave up his study of chemistry at college."],"answer":0,"explain":"主旨题。全文以 Perkin 从研究奎宁、意外得到紫色溶液到发现合成染料为线索，A 项概括最全。B 项只是第二段的一个背景细节；C 项是第三段用于对比的旧法；D 项与第五段“退学创业”相反。"},{"no":2,"stem":"Why did Perkin begin his experiments with aniline?","options":["A. To create a dye that would not fade in sunlight.","B. To test a scientific theory of Louis Pasteur.","C. To meet the growing demand for an inexpensive medicine.","D. To satisfy his teacher Thomas Hall's request for a new colour."],"answer":2,"explain":"细节题。定位第二段：1856 年奎宁供不应求，Perkin 想用便宜易得的煤焦油废料苯胺来制造奎宁。C 项“满足对廉价药品日益增长的需求”与之对应。A、D 项文中无据；B 项 Pasteur 的话是事后印证，不是实验目的。"},{"no":3,"stem":"What can be inferred about Perkin's discovery from the passage?","options":["A. It was the result of many years of careful planning.","B. It came from an experiment that failed to produce the intended result.","C. It was made possible by the support of his mentor Hofmann.","D. It was inspired by the purple colour of snail mucus."],"answer":1,"explain":"推断题。第二段说他“没能得到奎宁，反而得到一种神秘的黑色沉淀物”，紫色溶液是失败实验的意外产物，B 项正确。A 项与“意外”相反；C 项无据，后文反而是他违背 Hofmann 反对退学；D 项蜗牛紫色是后文对比自然染料价格的内容。"},{"no":4,"stem":"The word \"viable\" in Paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ____.","options":["A. cheap","B. workable","C. legal","D. popular"],"answer":1,"explain":"词义题。原句是“quinine was the only viable medical treatment for malaria”，viable 意为“可行的、能奏效的”。workable 最贴近。cheap（便宜）文中未说；legal（合法）、popular（流行）与语境不符。"},{"no":5,"stem":"What is the author's attitude towards Perkin's achievement?","options":["A. Doubtful.","B. Admiring.","C. Indifferent.","D. Critical."],"answer":1,"explain":"态度题。摘录用“scientific breakthrough”“would bring him both fame and fortune”“saw the potential of his unexpected find”等表述，并借 Pasteur 名言肯定他有准备的头脑，语气是赞赏的，选 B。A、D 项为负面，C 项为漠然，均不符。"}]},{"id":"reading-play-1","level":"cet4","title":"The Power of Play","source":{"textId":"u2c","unit":"u2","label":"游戏 Unit 2 · Text C · The Power of Play"},"passage":["Virtually every child, the world over, plays. The drive to play is so intense that children will do so in any circumstances, for instance when they have no real toys, or when parents do not actively encourage the behavior. In the eyes of a young child, running, pretending, and building are fun. Researchers and educators know that these playful activities benefit the development of the whole child across social, cognitive, physical, and emotional domains. Indeed play is such an instrumental component to healthy child development that the United Nations High Commission on Human Rights (1989) recognized play as a fundamental right of every child.","Yet, while experts continue to expound a powerful argument for the importance of play in children’s lives, the actual time children spend playing continues to decrease. Today, children play eight hours less each week than their counterparts did two decades ago (Elkind 2008). Under pressure of rising academic standards, play is being replaced by test preparation in kindergartens and grade schools, and parents who aim to give their preschoolers a leg up are led to believe that flashcards and educational ‘toys’ are the path to success. Our society has created a false dichotomy between play and learning. Through play, children learn to regulate their behavior, lay the foundations for later learning in science and mathematics, figure out the complex negotiations of social relationships, build a repertoire of creative problem-solving skills, and so much more. There is also an important role for adults in guiding children through playful learning opportunities.","Full consensus on a formal definition of play continues to elude the researchers and theorists who study it. A majority of the contemporary definitions of play focus on several key criteria. The founder of the National Institute for Play, Stuart Brown, has described play as anything that spontaneously is done for its own sake. More specifically, he says it ‘appears purposeless, produces pleasure and joy, [and] leads one to the next stage of mastery’ (as quoted in Tippett 2008). Similarly, Miller and Almon (2009) say that play includes ‘activities that are freely chosen and directed by children and arise from intrinsic motivation’."],"items":[{"no":1,"stem":"What does the passage mainly discuss?","options":["A. Why play is essential to children's development but is being reduced.","B. How parents can choose the best educational toys for preschoolers.","C. Why experts cannot agree on the definition of play.","D. How schools can prepare children for academic tests."],"answer":0,"explain":"主旨题。第一段讲游戏对儿童发展的价值与联合国确认的权利，第二段转向“游戏时间在减少、被应试准备取代”，A 项概括两者，最全面。B 项只是第二段批评的现象之一；C 项仅是第三段一个细节；D 项歪曲作者立场。"},{"no":2,"stem":"According to the passage, children today play ____.","options":["A. more creatively than children did in the past","B. about eight hours less per week than children two decades ago","C. with more educational toys than their parents did","D. in more organized groups than children two decades ago"],"answer":1,"explain":"细节题。定位第二段第二句：“children play eight hours less each week than their counterparts did two decades ago”，B 项为原文复现。A、C、D 项文中均无比较依据。"},{"no":3,"stem":"What does the author imply by saying \"Our society has created a false dichotomy between play and learning\"?","options":["A. Play and learning are in fact closely connected.","B. Learning should be given more time than play.","C. Society values play more than academic success.","D. Children cannot learn anything through play."],"answer":0,"explain":"推断题。该句后紧接“通过游戏，孩子学会调节行为、为科学和数学打基础……”，正说明游戏与学习并非对立，false dichotomy 是错误二分，A 项正确。B、C 项与原文倾向相反；D 项恰是作者要反驳的观点。"},{"no":4,"stem":"The word \"instrumental\" in Paragraph 1 most probably means ____.","options":["A. musical","B. harmful","C. very important","D. temporary"],"answer":2,"explain":"词义题。原句“play is such an instrumental component to healthy child development”中 instrumental 不指“乐器的”，而是“起重要作用的”，与 very important 相近。A 项是字面干扰；B、D 项不合语境。"},{"no":5,"stem":"What is the author's attitude toward replacing play with test preparation?","options":["A. Approving.","B. Concerned.","C. Amused.","D. Unclear."],"answer":1,"explain":"态度题。“under pressure of rising academic standards”“parents ... are led to believe”等措辞带批评意味，作者认为游戏被应试取代是问题，故为“担忧”，选 B。A 项赞赏、C 项觉得有趣、D 项不明，均不符。"}]},{"id":"reading-ants-1","level":"cet4","title":"Sparks of Intelligence in Ants","source":{"textId":"u5c","unit":"u5","label":"智力 Unit 5 · Text C · Ant Intelligence"},"passage":["When we think of intelligent members of the animal kingdom, the creatures that spring immediately to mind are apes and monkeys. But in fact the social lives of some members of the insect kingdom are sufficiently complex to suggest more than a hint of intelligence. Among these, the world of the ant has come in for considerable scrutiny lately, and the idea that ants demonstrate sparks of cognition has certainly not been rejected by those involved in these investigations. Ants store food, repel attackers and use chemical signals to contact one another in case of attack. Such chemical communication can be compared to the human use of visual and auditory channels (as in religious chants, advertising images and jingles, political slogans and martial music) to arouse and propagate moods and attitudes.","The biologist Lewis Thomas wrote, ‘Ants are so much like human beings as to be an embarrassment. They farm fungi, raise aphids as livestock, launch armies to war, use chemical sprays to alarm and confuse enemies, capture slaves, engage in child labour, exchange information ceaselessly. They do everything but watch television.’ However, in ants there is no cultural transmission—everything must be encoded in the genes—whereas in humans the opposite is true. Only basic instincts are carried in the genes of a newborn baby, other skills being learned from others in the community as the child grows up. It may seem that this cultural continuity gives us a huge advantage over ants.","They have never mastered fire nor progressed. Their fungus farming and aphid herding crafts are sophisticated when compared to the agricultural skills of humans five thousand years ago but have been totally overtaken by modern human agribusiness. The farming methods of ants are at least sustainable."],"items":[{"no":1,"stem":"What does the passage mainly discuss?","options":["A. Why ants are more intelligent than apes and monkeys.","B. Evidence suggesting that ants show a form of intelligence.","C. How ants communicate with each other by chemical signals.","D. Why ants have failed to make technological progress."],"answer":1,"explain":"主旨题。首段点明“the idea that ants demonstrate sparks of cognition has certainly not been rejected”，后文用化学信号、养殖真菌、可持续农业等证据支撑，B 项最全面。A 项拔高且与首句相悖；C 项只是其中一个方面；D 项是第三段开头的分句，不是主旨。"},{"no":2,"stem":"According to Lewis Thomas, ants do all of the following EXCEPT ____.","options":["A. farm fungi","B. raise aphids as livestock","C. watch television","D. exchange information ceaselessly"],"answer":2,"explain":"细节题。定位第二段 Lewis Thomas 的引文，末句是“They do everything but watch television”，即唯独不看电视，故选 C。A、B、D 项均在引文中直接列出。"},{"no":3,"stem":"What does the author imply by mentioning that ants have never mastered fire?","options":["A. Ants are unable to make any tools.","B. Human cultural transmission seems to give us an advantage over ants.","C. Fire is the most important human invention.","D. Ants are afraid of high temperatures."],"answer":1,"explain":"推断题。定位第二段末尾“It may seem that this cultural continuity gives us a huge advantage over ants”及第三段开头“They have never mastered fire nor progressed.”，作者用“未曾掌握火”说明人类文化传承看似占优，B 项正确。A 项过度绝对；C 项不是论证重点；D 项属无据臆测。"},{"no":4,"stem":"The word \"scrutiny\" in Paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ____.","options":["A. careful examination","B. strong criticism","C. public attention","D. scientific experiment"],"answer":0,"explain":"词义题。原句“the world of the ant has come in for considerable scrutiny lately”，下文列举多项研究，scrutiny 指“仔细的考察、审视”，选 A。B 项“批评”语气过重；C 项范围过宽；D 项特指实验，均不准确。"},{"no":5,"stem":"The author's attitude towards ants' achievements can best be described as ____.","options":["A. dismissive","B. respectful","C. pitying","D. puzzled"],"answer":1,"explain":"态度题。“magnificent work for ant lovers”“Even more impressively”“outstrip by far anything achieved by our distant ancestors”等用语带有明显敬意，选 B。A 项轻视、C 项怜悯、D 项困惑均与这些褒义表达不符。"}]},{"id":"reading-humour-1","level":"cet4","title":"What Makes Us Laugh","source":{"textId":"u7c","unit":"u7","label":"书籍 Unit 7 · Text C · What’s So Funny?"},"passage":["Theories about humour have an ancient pedigree. Plato expressed the idea that humour is simply a delighted feeling of superiority over others. Kant and Freud felt that joke-telling relies on building up a psychic tension which is safely punctured by the ludicrousness of the punchline. But most modern humour theorists have settled on some version of Aristotle’s belief that jokes are based on a reaction to or resolution of incongruity, when the punchline is either a nonsense or, though appearing silly, has a clever second meaning. Graeme Ritchie, a computational linguist in Edinburgh, studies the linguistic structure of jokes in order to understand not only humour but language understanding and reasoning in machines. He says that while there is no single format for jokes, many revolve around a sudden and surprising conceptual shift. A comedian will present a situation followed by an unexpected interpretation that is also apt. So even if a punchline sounds silly, the listener can see there is a clever semantic fit and that sudden mental ‘Aha!’ is the buzz that makes us laugh.","Viewed from this angle, humour is just a form of creative insight, a sudden leap to a new perspective. However, there is another type of laughter, the laughter of social appeasement and it is important to understand this too. Play is a crucial part of development in most young mammals. Rats produce ultrasonic squeaks to prevent their scuffles turning nasty. Chimpanzees have a ‘play-face’—a gaping expression accompanied by a panting ‘ah, ah’ noise.","In humans, these signals have mutated into smiles and laughs. Researchers believe social situations, rather than cognitive events such as jokes, trigger these instinctual markers of play or appeasement. People laugh on fairground rides or when tickled to flag a play situation, whether they feel amused or not. Both social and cognitive types of laughter tap into the same expressive machinery in our brains, the emotion and motor circuits that produce smiles and excited vocalisations."],"items":[{"no":1,"stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A. Two different explanations of why people laugh.","B. The history of humour theories from Plato to Freud.","C. How the brain reacts when a person hears a joke.","D. Why some jokes are funny and others are not."],"answer":0,"explain":"主旨题。摘录先讲“认知型笑”（笑话中的语义错位），再讲“社交型笑”（安抚与游戏信号），A 项概括两类解释，最全面。B 项只在开头一句带过；C 项是后文脑成像的内容，且不在本段摘录范围；D 项非全文重点。"},{"no":2,"stem":"According to the passage, most modern humour theorists believe jokes are based on ____.","options":["A. a feeling of superiority over others","B. the resolution of incongruity","C. a psychic tension built up and released","D. the imitation of animals at play"],"answer":1,"explain":"细节题。定位第一段：“most modern humour theorists have settled on some version of Aristotle's belief that jokes are based on a reaction to or resolution of incongruity”，B 项为原文改写。A 项是 Plato 的观点；C 项是 Kant 与 Freud 的看法；D 项无据。"},{"no":3,"stem":"What does Graeme Ritchie suggest about jokes?","options":["A. Every joke follows the same fixed format.","B. A punchline must be complete nonsense to be funny.","C. Jokes often involve a sudden shift in the listener's understanding.","D. Only experts can understand the structure of jokes."],"answer":2,"explain":"细节题。定位第一段后半：“many revolve around a sudden and surprising conceptual shift”，C 项与之对应。A 项与“no single format”矛盾；B 项原文是“nonsense or ... clever second meaning”二选一，不是必须无意义；D 项属过度推断。"},{"no":4,"stem":"In the phrase \"Viewed from this angle\", \"this angle\" refers to ____.","options":["A. the view that humour serves no biological purpose","B. the theory that a joke works through a clever semantic fit","C. the belief that animals laugh during play","D. the finding that laughter is a luxury reflex"],"answer":1,"explain":"指代题。定位前一句：“the listener can see there is a clever semantic fit and that sudden mental 'Aha!' is the buzz that makes us laugh”，所谓“这个角度”即“笑话靠巧妙的语义契合奏效”这一解释，B 项正确。A、D 项是 Koestler 的旧说；C 项属社交型笑的内容。"},{"no":5,"stem":"What is the author's attitude towards the social function of laughter?","options":["A. It is largely irrelevant to the study of humour.","B. It deserves attention alongside cognitive explanations.","C. It has been proved wrong by recent research.","D. It is more important than the cognitive explanation."],"answer":1,"explain":"态度题。作者用“it is important to understand this too”引出社交型笑，并指出两类笑“tap into the same expressive machinery”，说明社交功能与认知解释同等值得重视，B 项正确。A、C 项与原文相反；D 项“更重要”无依据。"}]}],"cloze":[{"id":"cloze-play-1","level":"cet4","title":"The Importance of Children's Play","source":{"textId":"u2b","unit":"u2","label":"游戏 Unit 2 · Text B · The Importance of Children's Play"},"passage":["Brick by brick, six-year-old Alice is building a __1__ kingdom. Imagining fairy-tale turrets fire-breathing dragons, wicked witches and gallant heroes, she’s creating an enchanting world. Although she isn’t aware of it, this fantasy is helping her take her first steps towards her __2__ for creativity and so it will have important repercussions in her adult life. Minutes later, Alice has __3__ the kingdom in favour of playing schools with her younger brother. When she bosses him around as his teacher, she’s practising how to __4__ her emotions through pretence. Later on, when they tire of this and settle down with a board game, she’s learning about the need to follow rules and take turns with a partner.","‘Play in all its rich variety is one of the highest achievements of the human species’, says Dr David Whitebread from the Faculty of Education at the University of Cambridge, UK. But we live in changing times, and Whitebread is mindful of a worldwide __5__ in play, pointing out that over half the people in the world now live in cities. International bodies like the United Nations and the European Union have begun to develop policies concerned with children’s right to play, and to consider __6__ for leisure facilities and educational programmes. But what they often __7__ is the __8__ to base policies on. ‘The type of play we are interested in is child-initiated, __9__ and unpredictable—but as soon as you ask a five-year-old “to play”, then you as the researcher have __10__,’ explains Dr. Sara Baker."],"options":{"A":"abandoned","B":"artificial","C":"capacity","D":"collapsed","E":"decline","F":"spontaneous","G":"evidence","H":"gathering","I":"implications","J":"intervened","K":"intervening","L":"lack","M":"magical","N":"regulate","O":"reputation"},"answers":{"1":"M","2":"C","3":"A","4":"N","5":"E","6":"I","7":"L","8":"G","9":"F","10":"J"},"explains":{"1":"此处需形容词修饰 kingdom。上文 fairy-tale turrets、dragons 等意象说明这是孩子想象出的“有魔力的”王国，选 magical；artificial 放在 a 之后冠词不合法。","2":"towards 后需名词，表示她“走向创造力”的起点。capacity for creativity 是常见搭配，指“创造的能力”，选 capacity。","3":"has 后需过去分词构成完成时。前文她正在搭王国，接着“几分钟后”就丢下去玩别的，abandoned（放弃）符合语意；collapsed 不及物，不能接 the kingdom。","4":"how to 后需动词原形，且与 emotions 搭配。regulate one's emotions 意为“调节情绪”，选 regulate。","5":"a worldwide ___ in play 需名词，且与下文“孩子游戏时间减少”一致，decline（下降）正确。","6":"consider 后需名词复数作宾语。implications for sth 意为“对……的影响”，与后文 policy、facilities 呼应，选 implications。","7":"what they often ___ is ... 中关系从句缺谓语，需动词原形。lack 意为“缺乏”，与后半句缺证据一致，选 lack。","8":"the evidence to base policies on 是固定表达“作为政策依据的证据”，需名词 evidence；-ing 形式不能替代。","9":"child-initiated, ___ and unpredictable 三个形容词并列，描述游戏特征。spontaneous（自发的）与 child-initiated 语义一致，选 spontaneous。","10":"have 后需过去分词。the researcher have intervened 意为“研究者已经介入”，与上文“你一让孩子去玩，研究就已介入”吻合，选 intervened；intervening 形式不合。"}},{"id":"cloze-glass-1","level":"cet4","title":"The History of Glass","source":{"textId":"u4b","unit":"u4","label":"历史 Unit 4 · Text B · The History of Glass"},"passage":["From our earliest origins, man has been making use of glass. Historians have discovered that a type of __1__ glass—obsidian—formed in places such as the mouth of a volcano as a result of the __2__ heat of an eruption of melting sand—was first used as tips for spears. Archaeologists have even found evidence of man-made glass which dates back to 4000 BC; this took the form of glazes used for coating stone beads. It was not until 1500 BC, however, that the first __3__ glass container was made by covering a sand core with a layer of molten glass.","Glass blowing became the most __4__ way to make glass containers from the first century BC. The glass made during this time was highly coloured due to the __5__ of the raw material. In the first century AD, methods of creating colourless glass were developed, which was then tinted by the __6__ of colouring materials. The __7__ of glass making was taken across Europe by the Romans during this century.","However, they guarded the skills and technology required to make glass very closely, and it was not until their empire __8__ in 476 AD that glass-making knowledge became __9__ throughout Europe and the Middle East. From the 10th century onwards, the Venetians gained a __10__ for technical skill and artistic ability in the making of glass bottles, and many of the city’s craftsmen left Italy to set up glassworks throughout Europe."],"options":{"A":"addition","B":"artificial","C":"collapsed","D":"common","E":"decline","F":"fragile","G":"hollow","H":"impurities","I":"intense","J":"measured","K":"natural","L":"pressure","M":"reputation","N":"secret","O":"widespread"},"answers":{"1":"K","2":"I","3":"G","4":"D","5":"H","6":"A","7":"N","8":"C","9":"O","10":"M"},"explains":{"1":"a type of ___ glass 需形容词，且后文说 obsidian 由火山高温自然形成，故为 natural glass（天然玻璃）；artificial 与 formed in a volcano 矛盾。","2":"the ___ heat of an eruption 需形容词修饰 heat。intense heat 是固定搭配，指“高温”，选 intense。","3":"the first ___ glass container 需形容词。早期玻璃容器是把熔融玻璃裹在沙芯外制成，中间是空的，hollow（中空的）正确；fragile 不合此处“首次出现空心容器”的史实重点。","4":"the most ___ way to make glass containers 需形容词。吹制法成为“最常见”的做法，common 与 most 搭配自然，选 common。","5":"due to the ___ of the raw material 需名词。生料含杂质使玻璃带色，impurities（杂质）正确，后文 colourless glass 的对比也支持。","6":"by the ___ of colouring materials 需名词。by the addition of ... 意为“通过添加……”，选 addition。","7":"The ___ of glass making 需名词。罗马人严密保守的正是制玻璃的“秘密”，后文 guarded ... very closely 与之呼应，选 secret。","8":"until their empire ___ in 476 AD 需一般过去时动词。帝国“崩溃”后技术才外传，collapsed 正确；measured 在此既无宾语也不合语意。","9":"became ___ throughout Europe 需形容词作表语。制玻璃知识“广泛传播”，widespread 正确。","10":"gained a ___ for technical skill 需名词。gain a reputation for 意为“因……获得声誉”，选 reputation。"}},{"id":"cloze-boredom-1","level":"cet4","title":"Why Being Bored Can Be Useful","source":{"textId":"u3c","unit":"u3","label":"动机 Unit 3 · Text C · Why Being Bored Is Stimulating—and Useful, too?"},"passage":["In his book, Boredom: A Lively History, Peter Toohey at the University of Calgary, Canada, compares it to disgust—an emotion that __1__ us to stay away from certain situations. ‘If disgust __2__ humans from infection, boredom may protect them from \"infectious\" social situations,’he suggests.","By asking people about their experiences of boredom, Thomas Goetz and his team at the University of Konstanz in Germany have recently __3__ five __4__ types: indifferent, calibrating, searching, reactant and apathetic. These can be plotted on two axes—one running left to right, which measures low to high arousal, and the other from top to bottom, which measures how positive or negative the feeling is. Intriguingly, Goetz has found that while people __5__ all kinds of boredom, they tend to __6__ in one.","Of the five types, the most __7__ is ‘reactant’ boredom with its explosive __8__ of high arousal and negative emotion. The most useful is what Goetz calls ‘indifferent’ boredom: someone isn’t __9__ in anything satisfying but still feels relaxed and calm. However, it remains to be seen whether there are any character traits that predict the kind of boredom each of us might be prone to.","Psychologist Sandi Mann at the University of Central Lancashire, UK, goes further. ‘All emotions are there for a reason, including boredom,’ she says. Mann has found that being bored makes us more __10__."],"options":{"A":"artificial","B":"combination","C":"creative","D":"damaging","E":"distinct","F":"engaged","G":"experience","H":"identified","I":"motivates","J":"predicting","K":"pressure","L":"protected","M":"protects","N":"specialise","O":"temporary"},"answers":{"1":"I","2":"M","3":"H","4":"E","5":"G","6":"N","7":"D","8":"B","9":"F","10":"C"},"explains":{"1":"an emotion that ___ us to stay away 需第三人称单数谓语。motivate sb to do 意为“促使某人做某事”，选 motivates；protects 后不能接 to stay away 这种结构。","2":"If disgust ___ humans from infection 需第三人称单数谓语。protect sb from sth 意为“保护某人免受……”，选 protects；motivates 与 from 搭配不成立。","3":"have recently ___ five distinct types 需过去分词。identify 意为“识别出”，此处指研究者“划分出五类”，选 identified。","4":"five ___ types 需形容词。后文列出 indifferent、searching 等互不相同的类别，distinct（截然不同的）正确；damaging 与分类无关。","5":"while people ___ all kinds of boredom 需动词原形。experience 意为“经历、体验”，与 all kinds of boredom 搭配，选 experience。","6":"they tend to ___ in one 需动词原形，且能与 in 搭配。specialise in 意为“专长于”，指人往往只对某一种无聊最敏感，选 specialise。","7":"the most ___ is 'reactant' boredom 需形容词。后文对举 the most useful，此处应是“最有害的”，damaging 正确。","8":"its explosive ___ of high arousal and negative emotion 需名词。combination of A and B 指“高唤醒与负面情绪的组合”，选 combination。","9":"isn't ___ in anything satisfying 需过去分词。be engaged in 意为“投入、参与”，与“没在做令人满足的事”呼应，选 engaged。","10":"makes us more ___ 需形容词。本段主旨是无聊能激发创造力，creative 正确；temporary、artificial 均不合语意。"}}],"matching":[{"id":"matching-henderson","level":"cet4","title":"Alexander Henderson: A Landscape Photographer","source":{"textId":"u1b","unit":"u1","label":"传记 Unit 1 · Text B · Alexander Henderson (1831-1913)"},"passage":[{"label":"A","text":"Born in Scotland, Henderson emigrated to Canada in 1855 and became a well-known landscape photographer Alexander Henderson was born in Scotland in 1831 and was the son of a successful merchant. His grandfather, also called Alexander, had founded the family business, and later became the first chairman of the National Bank of Scotland. The family had extensive landholdings in Scotland. Besides its residence in Edinburgh, it owned Press Estate, 650 acres of farmland about 35 miles southeast of the city."},{"label":"B","text":"The family often stayed at Press Castle, the large mansion on the northern edge of the property, and Alexander spent much of his childhood in the area, playing on the beach near Eyemouth or fishing in the streams nearby. Even after he went to school at Murcheston Academy on the outskirts of Edinburgh, Henderson returned to Press at weekends. In 1849 he began a three-year apprenticeship to become an accountant."},{"label":"C","text":"Although he never liked the prospect of a business career, he stayed with it to please his family. In October 1855, however, he emigrated to Canada with his wife Agnes Elder Robertson and they settled in Montreal. Henderson learned photography in Montreal around the year 1857 and quickly took it up as a serious amateur. He became a personal friend and colleague of the Scottish-Canadian photographer William Notman."},{"label":"D","text":"The two men made a photographic excursion to Niagara Falls in 1860 and they cooperated on experiments with magnesium flares as a source of artificial light in 1865. They belonged to the same societies and were among the founding members of the Art Association of Montreal. Henderson acted as chairman of the association’s first meeting, which was held in Notman’s studio on 11 January 1860. In spite of their friendship, their styles of photography were quite different. While Notman’s landscapes were noted for their bold realism, Henderson for the first 20 years of his career produced romantic images, showing the strong influence of the British landscape tradition."},{"label":"E","text":"His artistic and technical progress was rapid and in 1865 he published his first major collection of landscape photographs. The publication had limited circulation (only seven copies have ever been found) and was called Canadian Views and Studies. The contents of each copy vary significantly and have proved a useful source for evaluating Henderson’s early work. In 1866, he gave up his business to open a photographic studio, advertising himself as a portrait and landscape photographer."},{"label":"F","text":"From about 1870 he dropped portraiture to specialize in landscape photography and other views. There was sufficient demand for these types of scenes and others he took depicting the lumber trade, steamboats and waterfalls to enable him to make a living. There was little competing hobby or amateur photography before the late 1880s because of the time-consuming techniques involved and the weight of the equipment. People wanted to buy photographs as souvenirs of a trip or as gifts, and catering to this market, Henderson had stock photographs on display at his studio for mounting, framing, or inclusion in albums."},{"label":"G","text":"Henderson frequently exhibited his photographs in Montreal and abroad, in London, Edinburgh, Dublin, Paris, New York, and Philadelphia. He met with greater success in 1877 and 1878 in New York when he won first prizes in the exhibition held by E and HT Anthony and Company for landscapes using the Lambertype process. In 1878 his work won second prize at the world exhibition in Paris. In the 1870s and 1880s Henderson travelled widely throughout Quebec and Ontario, in Canada documenting the major cities of the two provinces and many of the villages in Quebec."},{"label":"H","text":"He was especially fond of the wilderness and often travelled by canoe on the Blanche, du Lièvre and other noted eastern rivers. He went on several occasions to the Maritimes and in 1872 he sailed by yacht along the lower north shore of the St Lawrence River. That same year, while in the lower St Lawrence River region, he took some photographs of the construction of the Intercolonial Railway. This undertaking led in 1875 to a commission from the railway to record the principal structures along the almost-completed line connecting Montreal to Halifax."},{"label":"I","text":"Commissions from other railways followed. In 1876 he photographed bridges on the Quebec, Montreal, Ottawa and Occidental Railway between Montreal and Ottawa. In 1885 he went west along the Canadian Pacific Railway (CPR) as far as Rogers Pass in British Columbia, where he took photographs of mountains and the progress of construction. In 1892 Henderson accepted a full-time position with the CPR as manager of a photographic department which he was to set up and administer."},{"label":"J","text":"His duties included spending four months in the field each year. That summer he made his second trip west, photographing extensively along the railway line as far as Victoria. He continued in this post until 1897, when he retired completely from photography. When Henderson died in 1913, his huge collection of glass negatives was stored in the basement of his house. Today collections of his work are held at the National Archives of Canada, Ottawa, and the McCord Museum of Canadian History, Montreal."}],"items":[{"no":1,"statement":"In Montreal he worked with another photographer on projects, although the two men's styles were not the same.","answer":"D","explain":"对应 D 段。D 段写他与 Notman 1860 年同游尼亚加拉瀑布、1865 年合作实验镁光，并同为蒙特利尔艺术协会创始成员；同段末句还点出两人风格不同（Notman 写实，Henderson 浪漫）。"},{"no":2,"statement":"He was drawn to wild places, often travelling by canoe, and he photographed a railway being built.","answer":"H","explain":"对应 H 段。H 段写他喜爱荒野、常乘独木舟沿 Blanche 等河旅行，1872 年乘游艇沿圣劳伦斯河而下，同年拍摄 Intercolonial Railway 的修建。"},{"no":3,"statement":"Henderson came from a well-off Scottish family whose wealth included a large country estate and a link with banking.","answer":"A","explain":"对应 A 段。A 段提到祖父创办家族企业并任 National Bank of Scotland 首任主席，家族拥有 Press Estate 等大片地产。B 段虽写庄园生活，但没有银行信息，故不选 B。"},{"no":4,"statement":"After his death his collection of negatives was kept in Canadian public institutions.","answer":"J","explain":"对应 J 段。J 段写 1913 年他去世时玻璃底片堆在家中地下室，如今藏品分藏于加拿大国家档案馆（渥太华）与麦科德博物馆（蒙特利尔）。"},{"no":5,"statement":"He earned his living from a photographic studio and eventually gave up portrait work for landscape photography.","answer":"F","explain":"对应 F 段。F 段写 1866 年弃商开照相馆，约 1870 年起放弃人像、专攻风景，并靠市场需求谋生。"},{"no":6,"statement":"As a child he spent much of his time on the family land, playing on the beach and fishing in nearby streams.","answer":"B","explain":"对应 B 段。B 段写明他童年在 Press Castle 一带度过，在 Eyemouth 附近海滩玩耍、在溪边钓鱼。A 段只讲家族产业，不含这些童年活动。"},{"no":7,"statement":"Railway companies repeatedly commissioned him, and one of them eventually employed him full time.","answer":"I","explain":"对应 I 段。I 段说铁路公司的委托接踵而来，1892 年他接受 CPR 的全职职位，负责筹建并管理摄影部门。"},{"no":8,"statement":"He finished training for a career he never wanted, and then left for Canada with his wife.","answer":"C","explain":"对应 C 段。C 段说 1849 年起做三年会计学徒，“never liked the prospect of a business career”却为取悦家人坚持下来，1855 年 10 月携妻移民加拿大。D 段开始讲在加拿大的摄影活动，时间上更晚。"},{"no":9,"statement":"His work was shown internationally and received prizes in New York and Paris.","answer":"G","explain":"对应 G 段。G 段写他在蒙特利尔及伦敦、巴黎、纽约等地办展，1877–1878 年在纽约获头奖，1878 年在巴黎世界博览会获二等奖。"},{"no":10,"statement":"His first published collection of landscape photographs is extremely rare today.","answer":"E","explain":"对应 E 段。E 段说 1865 年出版第一部重要风景摄影集 Canadian Views and Studies，“only seven copies have ever been found”。F 段讲 1866 年开照相馆，与“出版摄影集”不是同一件事。"}]},{"id":"matching-iconoclast","level":"cet4","title":"How Iconoclasts Think Differently","source":{"textId":"u5b","unit":"u5","label":"智力 Unit 5 · Text B · A Neuroscientist Reveals How to Think Differently"},"passage":[{"label":"A","text":"In the last decade a revolution has occurred in the way that scientists think about the brain. We now know that the decisions humans make can be traced to the firing patterns of neurons in specific parts of the brain. These discoveries have led to the field known as neuroeconomics, which studies the brain’s secrets to success in an economic environment that demands innovation and being able to do things differently from competitors."},{"label":"B","text":"A brain that can do this is an iconoclastic one. Briefly, an iconoclast is a person who does something that others say can’t be done. This definition implies that iconoclasts are different from other people, but more precisely, it is their brains that are different in three distinct ways: perception, fear response, and social intelligence. Each of these three functions utilizes a different circuit in the brain."},{"label":"C","text":"Naysayers might suggest that the brain is irrelevant, that thinking in an original, even revolutionary, way is more a matter of personality than brain function. But the field of neuroeconomics was born out of the realization that the physical workings of the brain place limitations on the way we make decisions. By understanding these constraints, we begin to understand why some people march to a different drumbeat. The first thing to realize is that the brain suffers from limited resources."},{"label":"D","text":"It has a fixed energy budget, about the same as a 40 watt light bulb, so it has evolved to work as efficiently as possible. This is where most people are impeded from being an iconoclast. For example, when confronted with information streaming from the eyes, the brain will interpret this information in the quickest way possible. Thus it will draw on both past experience and any other source of information, such as what other people say, to make sense of what it is seeing. This happens all the time."},{"label":"E","text":"The brain takes shortcuts that work so well we are hardly ever aware of them. We think our perceptions of the world are real, but they are only biological and electrical rumblings. Perception is not simply a product of what your eyes or ears transmit to your brain. More than the physical reality of photons or sound waves, perception is a product of the brain. Perception is central to iconoclasm."},{"label":"F","text":"Iconoclasts see things differently to other people. Their brains do not fall into efficiency pitfalls as much as the average person’s brain. Iconoclasts, either because they were born that way or through learning, have found ways to work around the perceptual shortcuts that plague most people. Perception is not something that is hardwired into the brain. It is a learned process, which is both a curse and an opportunity for change. The brain faces the fundamental problem of interpreting physical stimuli from the senses."},{"label":"G","text":"Everything the brain sees, hears, or touches has multiple interpretations. The one that is ultimately chosen is simply the brain’s best theory. In technical terms, these conjectures have their basis in the statistical likelihood of one interpretation over another and are heavily influenced by past experience and, importantly for potential iconoclasts, what other people say. The best way to see things differently to other people is to bombard the brain with things it has never encountered before. Novelty releases the perceptual process from the chains of past experience and forces the brain to make new judgments. Successful iconoclasts have an extraordinary willingness to be exposed to what is fresh and different."},{"label":"H","text":"Observation of iconoclasts shows that they embrace novelty while most people avoid things that are different. The problem with novelty, however, is that it tends to trigger the brain’s fear system. Fear is a major impediment to thinking like an iconoclast and stops the average person in his tracks. There are many types of fear, but the two that inhibit iconoclastic thinking and people generally find difficult to deal with are fear of uncertainty and fear of public ridicule. These may seem like trivial phobias. But fear of public speaking, which everyone must do from time to time, afflicts one-third of the population."},{"label":"I","text":"This makes it too common to be considered a mental disorder. It is simply a common variant of human nature, one which iconoclasts do not let inhibit their reactions. Finally, to be successful iconoclasts, individuals must sell their ideas to other people. This is where social intelligence comes in. Social intelligence is the ability to understand and manage people in a business setting. In the last decade there has been an explosion of knowledge about the social brain and how the brain works when groups coordinate decision making. Neuroscience has revealed which brain circuits are responsible for functions like understanding what other people think, empathy, fairness, and social identity."},{"label":"J","text":"These brain regions play key roles in whether people convince others of their ideas. Perception is important in social cognition too. The perception of someone’s enthusiasm, or reputation, can make or break a deal. Understanding how perception becomes intertwined with social decision making shows why successful iconoclasts are so rare. Iconoclasts create new opportunities in every area from artistic expression to technology to business. They supply creativity and innovation not easily accomplished by committees. Rules aren’t important to them. Iconoclasts face alienation and failure, but can also be a major asset to any organization. It is crucial for success in any field to understand how the iconoclastic mind works."}],"items":[{"no":1,"statement":"To see things in a new way, the brain must meet unfamiliar things that weaken the influence of past experience.","answer":"G","explain":"对应 G 段。G 段说最好的办法是“bombard the brain with things it has never encountered before”，novelty 能“releases the perceptual process from the chains of past experience”。"},{"no":2,"statement":"Although some people doubt that brain science matters, the writer argues that it helps explain why a few individuals act so differently.","answer":"C","explain":"对应 C 段。C 段先提 naysayers 认为思考方式“more a matter of personality than brain function”，再用 But 转折说明神经经济学正是要解释为什么有人“march to a different drumbeat”。"},{"no":3,"statement":"Iconoclasts may face rejection and failure, but organizations still benefit greatly from their originality.","answer":"J","explain":"对应 J 段。J 段说 iconoclasts“face alienation and failure, but can also be a major asset to any organization”，并提供难以由委员会替代的创造力。"},{"no":4,"statement":"What we see is constructed by the brain and is not a direct copy of what our senses receive.","answer":"E","explain":"对应 E 段。E 段说“We think our perceptions of the world are real, but they are only biological and electrical rumblings”，知觉是大脑的产物而非感官的直接传输。"},{"no":5,"statement":"New research connects human decisions with the activity of particular brain cells, and this has led to a new area of study.","answer":"A","explain":"对应 A 段。A 段说决策可追溯到特定脑区神经元的放电模式，由此产生 neuroeconomics（神经经济学）。B 段开始定义 iconoclast，不是“新学科”的出处。"},{"no":6,"statement":"New experiences tend to arouse fear, especially fear of uncertainty and of being laughed at.","answer":"H","explain":"对应 H 段。H 段说 novelty 往往触发恐惧系统，最难处理的是 fear of uncertainty 和 fear of public ridicule。"},{"no":7,"statement":"Because the brain can spend only a limited amount of energy, it interprets incoming information as quickly as it can, drawing on past experience.","answer":"D","explain":"对应 D 段。D 段写大脑“fixed energy budget, about the same as a 40 watt light bulb”，于是以最快方式解读眼睛传来的信息，并借助过去经验与他人说法。E 段讲的是知觉的性质，不是能量预算。"},{"no":8,"statement":"Fear of public speaking is so common that it is not an illness, and social intelligence helps iconoclasts persuade others.","answer":"I","explain":"对应 I 段。I 段说公开演讲恐惧影响三分之一人口，“too common to be considered a mental disorder”，只是一种 human nature 的常见变体；随后讲 social intelligence 与说服他人的脑区。J 段讲的是 iconoclast 的组织价值，不是这一层。"},{"no":9,"statement":"In the writer's view, an iconoclast manages to do what others consider impossible, and the difference lies in three brain functions.","answer":"B","explain":"对应 B 段。B 段给出 iconoclast 定义“does something that others say can't be done”，并指出其大脑在 perception、fear response、social intelligence 三方面不同。"},{"no":10,"statement":"Unlike most people, iconoclasts have learned to avoid the mental shortcuts that limit perception.","answer":"F","explain":"对应 F 段。F 段说普通人的大脑总走捷径，而 iconoclasts“have found ways to work around the perceptual shortcuts”，且知觉是可习得的。"}]}],"translation":[{"id":"trans-2024-12-nev","level":"cet4","title":"2024年12月 · 新能源汽车产业","zh":"近年来，中国新能源汽车产业发展迅速。目前，中国新能源汽车年产量已高达近千万辆，占全球市场份额超过60%，出口量不断创出新高。中国政府通过加大资金投入和政策引导，鼓励新能源汽车企业进行技术创新，不断提高产品在市场上的竞争力。中国新能源汽车产业的发展不仅有力推动了国内经济发展，也为全球新能源利用和环境保护做出了积极贡献。","reference":"In recent years, China's new energy vehicles industry has been developing rapidly. At present, China's annual output of new energy vehicles has reached nearly 10 million units, accounting for more than 60 percent of the global market share, and the volume of exports has continued to reach new highs. The Chinese government encourages new energy vehicle enterprises to innovate technology and improve the competitiveness of their products in the market by increasing capital investment and policy guidance. The development of China's new energy vehicle industry not only vigorously promotes domestic economic development, but also makes positive contributions to the global utilization of new energy and environmental protection.","keyPhrases":[{"en":"new energy vehicles","zh":"新能源汽车"},{"en":"annual output","zh":"年产量"},{"en":"account for","zh":"占（比例）"},{"en":"market share","zh":"市场份额"},{"en":"reach new highs","zh":"创出新高"},{"en":"capital investment","zh":"资金投入"},{"en":"policy guidance","zh":"政策引导"},{"en":"make positive contributions to","zh":"为……做出积极贡献"}],"tips":["数据表达：\"近千万辆\"译作 nearly 10 million units；\"超过60%\"用 accounting for more than 60 percent 作伴随状语，避免另起一句。","\"通过加大资金投入和政策引导\"处理为 by increasing ... and ...，方式状语后置，符合英文习惯。","\"不仅……也……\"用 not only ... but also ... 连接两个谓语，注意前后结构对称。"],"source":"2024年12月四级翻译真题"},{"id":"trans-2024-12-mogao","level":"cet4","title":"2024年12月 · 莫高窟数字展示中心","zh":"敦煌莫高窟数字展示中心于2014年开放启用，是莫高窟保护利用工程的重要组成部分。展示中心采用数字技术和多媒体展示手段，使游客进入洞窟参观之前就能了解莫高窟的历史文化，鉴赏莫高窟的艺术经典。这将减少开放洞窟的数量，缩短游客在洞窟内的逗留时间，减轻参观对莫高窟造成的影响，以使这一世界文化遗产得到妥善保护、长久利用。","reference":"The Dunhuang Mogao Grottoes Digital Exhibition Center, opened in 2014, is an important part of the protection and utilization project of the Mogao Grottoes. The exhibition center uses digital technology and multimedia display methods to enable tourists to understand the history and culture of the Mogao Grottoes and appreciate the artistic classics of the Mogao Grottoes before entering the caves for a visit. This will reduce the number of caves open, shorten the stay time of tourists in the caves, and reduce the impact of visits to the Mogao Caves, so that this world cultural heritage can be properly protected and used for a long time.","keyPhrases":[{"en":"digital exhibition center","zh":"数字展示中心"},{"en":"protection and utilization project","zh":"保护利用工程"},{"en":"multimedia display","zh":"多媒体展示"},{"en":"appreciate the artistic classics","zh":"鉴赏艺术经典"},{"en":"stay time","zh":"逗留时间"},{"en":"reduce the impact","zh":"减轻影响"},{"en":"world cultural heritage","zh":"世界文化遗产"},{"en":"be properly protected","zh":"得到妥善保护"}],"tips":["首句用过去分词短语 opened in 2014 作插入语，全句只有一个谓语 is，避免逐字对译出现两个动词。","\"使游客……就能……\"用 enable sb. to do 结构；\"进入洞窟参观之前\"用 before entering the caves 分词短语。","连续三个动作 reduce ..., shorten ..., and reduce ... 用并列谓语，最后 so that 引导目的状语。"],"source":"2024年12月四级翻译真题"},{"id":"trans-2024-06-siheyuan","level":"cet4","title":"2024年6月 · 四合院","zh":"四合院是中国一种传统的住宅建筑，其特点是房屋建造在一个院子的四周，将院子合围在中间。四合院通常冬暖夏凉，环境舒适，尤其适合大家庭居住。四合院在中国各地有多种类型，其中以北京的四合院最为典型。如今，随着现代城市的发展，传统的四合院已逐渐减少，但因其独特的建筑风格，四合院对中国文化的传承和中国历史的研究具有重要意义。","reference":"Siheyuan, a kind of traditional Chinese residential building, is characterized by its layout where houses are built around a courtyard, enclosing it in four directions. Usually warm in winter and cool in summer, siheyuan offers a comfortable environment, especially suitable for large families to live in. There are various types of siheyuan across China, among which siheyuan in Beijing is the most typical. Today, with the development of modern cities, the number of traditional siheyuan has gradually diminished. However, due to its unique architectural style, siheyuan holds great significance for the preservation of Chinese culture and the study of Chinese history.","keyPhrases":[{"en":"traditional Chinese residential building","zh":"中国传统住宅建筑"},{"en":"be characterized by","zh":"以……为特点"},{"en":"enclose","zh":"合围"},{"en":"warm in winter and cool in summer","zh":"冬暖夏凉"},{"en":"be suitable for","zh":"适合"},{"en":"gradually diminish","zh":"逐渐减少"},{"en":"due to","zh":"由于"},{"en":"hold great significance for","zh":"对……具有重要意义"}],"tips":["\"其特点是……\"用 be characterized by + 名词短语，比 its feature is that 更符合英文表达。","\"冬暖夏凉，环境舒适\"可用 warm in winter and cool in summer 作前置形容词短语，其逻辑主语仍是 siheyuan。","\"随着……的发展\"用 with the development of；末句用 However 转折，due to 引原因，主句谓语用 holds。"],"source":"2024年6月四级翻译真题"},{"id":"trans-2024-06-lunar-calendar","level":"cet4","title":"2024年6月 · 农历","zh":"农历起源于数千年前的中国，根据太阳和月亮的运行规律制定。长期以来，农历在农业生产和人们日常生活中发挥着重要作用。古人依据农历记录日期、安排农活，以便最有效地利用自然资源和气候条件，提高农作物的产量和质量。中国的春节、中秋节等传统节日的日期都基于农历。农历是中国传统文化的重要组成部分，当今依然广为使用。","reference":"The lunar calendar, which originated in China thousands of years ago, was formulated based on the movement patterns of both the sun and the moon. For a long time, it has played a significant role in agricultural production and people's daily life. Ancient people used the lunar calendar to record dates and schedule their agricultural activities, in order to make the most effective use of natural resources and climatic conditions, thereby increasing the yield and quality of crops. The dates of traditional Chinese festivals such as the Spring Festival and the Mid-Autumn Festival are all based on the lunar calendar. As an integral part of the traditional Chinese culture, the lunar calendar is still widely used today.","keyPhrases":[{"en":"lunar calendar","zh":"农历"},{"en":"originate in","zh":"起源于"},{"en":"movement patterns","zh":"运行规律"},{"en":"play a significant role in","zh":"在……中发挥重要作用"},{"en":"agricultural production","zh":"农业生产"},{"en":"climatic conditions","zh":"气候条件"},{"en":"yield","zh":"产量"},{"en":"be based on","zh":"基于"}],"tips":["首句用 which 引导的非限制性定语从句补充\"起源于\"，主句用被动 was formulated，避免两个谓语并列。","\"以便……从而提高……\"用 in order to + 动词原形，再由 thereby + 现在分词衔接，比连写两句更紧凑。","\"重要组成部分\"用 (an) integral part of；注意 be based on 后接名词，不接从句。"],"source":"2024年6月四级翻译真题"},{"id":"trans-2024-06-fu-character","level":"cet4","title":"2024年6月 · “福”字","zh":"汉语中的“福”字表示幸福和好运，是中国传统文化中最常用的吉祥符号之一。人们通常将一个大大的福字写在红纸上，寓意期盼家庭幸福、社会安定、国家昌盛。春节贴福字是民间由来已久的习俗。为了欢庆春节，家家户户都会将福字贴在门上或墙上，表达对幸福生活的向往、对美好未来的期待。人们有时还将福字倒过来贴，表示幸福已到、好运已到。","reference":"The Chinese character fu, representing happiness and good fortune, ranks among the most commonly used auspicious symbols in traditional Chinese culture. People usually write a large character fu on the red paper, symbolizing the hope for family happiness, social stability, and national prosperity. Pasting the character fu during the Spring Festival is a long-standing folk custom. To celebrate the festival, every household pastes the character fu on doors or walls, expressing their aspiration for a happy life and their hope for a bright future. At times, the character fu is even pasted upside down, which signifies the arrival of happiness and good luck.","keyPhrases":[{"en":"Chinese character","zh":"汉字"},{"en":"auspicious symbols","zh":"吉祥符号"},{"en":"rank among","zh":"位居……之列"},{"en":"national prosperity","zh":"国家昌盛"},{"en":"long-standing folk custom","zh":"由来已久的民间习俗"},{"en":"every household","zh":"家家户户"},{"en":"upside down","zh":"倒过来"},{"en":"signify","zh":"表示；意味着"}],"tips":["\"福\"首次出现用 the Chinese character fu，后文可简作 the character fu，注意定冠词的重复使用。","\"寓意……\"用现在分词 symbolizing 作伴随状语，不必另起一句，避免句式松散。","\"倒过来贴\"译作 is pasted upside down，用被动语态；which signifies ... 用非限制性定语从句补充含义。"],"source":"2024年6月四级翻译真题"},{"id":"trans-2023-12-healthy-diet","level":"cet4","title":"2023年12月 · 健康饮食","zh":"中国政府十分重视人民的健康饮食。通过大力提倡健康饮食，人们对合理营养增进健康的重要性有了更加深刻的认识。“吃得安全、吃得营养、吃得健康”是人民对美好生活的基本需要，是提升人民幸福感的必然要求，也为食品产业的发展提供了新机遇。目前，各级政府都在采取多种举措确保人民饮食健康，推进健康中国的建设。","reference":"The Chinese government lays great emphasis on the healthy diet for its people. Through its advocation of healthy diet, people have had a deeper understanding of the significance of proper nutrition for promoting their health. Eating safely, nutritionally and healthily is people's basic need for a better life and the inevitable requirement for enhancing their sense of happiness. It also provides new opportunities for the development of the food industry. Currently, governments at all levels are taking a variety of measures to ensure that people have a healthy diet and promote the construction of a healthy China.","keyPhrases":[{"en":"lay great emphasis on","zh":"十分重视"},{"en":"healthy diet","zh":"健康饮食"},{"en":"proper nutrition","zh":"合理营养"},{"en":"significance","zh":"重要性"},{"en":"inevitable requirement","zh":"必然要求"},{"en":"sense of happiness","zh":"幸福感"},{"en":"governments at all levels","zh":"各级政府"},{"en":"a variety of measures","zh":"多种举措"}],"tips":["\"十分重视\"除 attach importance to 外，lay emphasis on 后接名词更简洁；注意 emphasis 的拼写。","\"吃得安全、吃得营养、吃得健康\"用副词并列 Eating safely, nutritionally and healthily，避免重复动词。","\"是……是……也为……\"注意三部分并列，谓语分别用 is / is / provides，最后一个主语 it 指代前面整个行为。"],"source":"2023年12月四级翻译真题"},{"id":"trans-2023-06-lifelong-education","level":"cet4","title":"2023年6月 · 终身教育与继续教育","zh":"中国越来越重视终身教育，发展继续教育是构建终身教育体系的有效途径。高校作为人才培养的基地，拥有先进的教学理念和优越的教学资源，理应成为继续教育的办学主体。因此，近年来许多高校适应社会需求，加强与用人单位沟通，努力探索一条符合中国国情的继续教育发展新路，以使继续教育在国家发展战略中发挥更大的作用。","reference":"China attaches more and more importance to lifelong education, and the development of continuing education is an effective way to build a lifelong education system. As the base for talent cultivation, universities have advanced teaching concepts and superior teaching resources, so they should become the main providers of continuing education. Therefore, in recent years, a number of universities have been trying to explore a new path for developing continuing education that suits China's specific conditions by catering to social needs and strengthening communications with employers, so that continuing education can play a greater role in the national development strategy.","keyPhrases":[{"en":"lifelong education","zh":"终身教育"},{"en":"continuing education","zh":"继续教育"},{"en":"effective way","zh":"有效途径"},{"en":"talent cultivation","zh":"人才培养"},{"en":"teaching concepts","zh":"教学理念"},{"en":"main providers","zh":"办学主体"},{"en":"specific conditions","zh":"国情"},{"en":"play a greater role","zh":"发挥更大的作用"}],"tips":["\"越来越重视\"用 attach more and more importance to，注意 more and more 修饰 importance，不要写成 attach importance more。","\"作为……基地\"用 As the base for ... 作状语；后半句用 so 连接因果，不要用逗号硬连两个完整句。","\"适应社会需求、加强与用人单位沟通\"用 by doing 结构作方式状语；目的用 so that 引导从句。"],"source":"2023年6月四级翻译真题"},{"id":"trans-2023-06-compulsory-education","level":"cet4","title":"2023年6月 · 义务教育","zh":"中国政府一直大力推行义务教育，以使每个儿童都享有受教育的机会。自1986年《义务教育法》生效以来，经过不懈努力，实现了在全国推行义务教育的目标。如今，在中国，儿童年满六周岁开始上小学，从小学到初中一共接受九年义务教育。从2008年秋季学期开始，义务教育阶段学生无须缴纳学费。随着一系列教育改革举措的实施，中国义务教育的质量也有显著提高。","reference":"The Chinese government has been vigorously carrying out compulsory education all the time so that every child can have access to education. Since the Compulsory Education Law came into effect in 1986, the government has worked diligently to achieve the goal of making compulsory education available throughout the country. Today, children in China, starting school at the age of six, receive a total of nine years of compulsory education from primary school to junior high school. Since the fall semester of 2008, students during compulsory education don't have to pay tuition fees. With the implementation of a series of educational reform measures, the quality of China's compulsory education has also been significantly improved.","keyPhrases":[{"en":"compulsory education","zh":"义务教育"},{"en":"have access to education","zh":"享有受教育机会"},{"en":"come into effect","zh":"生效"},{"en":"work diligently","zh":"不懈努力"},{"en":"make ... available","zh":"使……得以实现"},{"en":"junior high school","zh":"初中"},{"en":"tuition fees","zh":"学费"},{"en":"education reform","zh":"教育改革"}],"tips":["\"一直大力推行\"用现在完成进行时 has been carrying out，体现从过去持续到现在。","\"自……生效以来\"用 Since + 一般过去时从句，主句用现在完成时 has worked。","\"实现……的目标\"用 achieve the goal of doing；\"无须缴纳学费\"用 don't have to pay，不用 mustn't。"],"source":"2023年6月四级翻译真题"}],"writing":[{"id":"write-social-media","level":"cet4","title":"Social Media and Campus Relationships","topic":"Some people believe that social media helps college students expand their social circles. Others argue that it makes students more isolated in real-life relationships. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Do you agree or disagree that social media has a positive impact on college students' social relationships? Give reasons and examples to support your opinion.","cn":"讨论社交媒体对大学生人际关系的影响：有人认为它扩大社交圈，有人认为它使人更疏离；兼顾两种观点并给出自己的判断。","requirements":["120–180 words","discuss both views","state your own opinion","support with reasons and examples"],"outline":["引入：社交媒体已成为大学生日常交流的一部分，但它让人更近还是更远？","主体：一方说它打破地域、找到同好；另一方说点赞代替了深谈、比较带来焦虑。","结尾：给出自己的判断——关键不在工具，而在使用的分寸。"],"model":"When it comes to social media, college students are often divided. Some argue that platforms like WeChat help us find friends who share our interests, even far away. Others insist that screen time leaves us more alone, because a stream of likes is no substitute for a long talk over tea. In my view, both sides have a point. Social media is a double-edged sword. It opens a door to new communities, but it can also turn friendship into a performance, where we compare lives and feel anxious. I have seen classmates spend an evening refreshing feeds instead of joining a discussion in the dorm. Therefore, the question is not whether to use these tools, but how. If we set aside time for face-to-face communication and treat social media as a way to arrange meetings rather than replace them, it can serve us instead of ruling us.","phrases":[{"en":"when it comes to","zh":"谈到……"},{"en":"on the one hand ... on the other hand ...","zh":"一方面……另一方面……"},{"en":"a double-edged sword","zh":"双刃剑"},{"en":"strike a balance between A and B","zh":"在 A 与 B 之间取得平衡"},{"en":"face-to-face communication","zh":"面对面交流"},{"en":"it is undeniable that","zh":"不可否认……"}],"source":"写作练习材料《Writing Topics for practice(1).docx》（北师大珠海大一英语课程，真实题干）"},{"id":"write-competition-cooperation","level":"cet4","title":"Competition or Cooperation on Campus","topic":"Some people hold that competition among university students improves learning efficiency. Others believe that cooperation is more important for college students' growth. Do you agree or disagree that competition is more beneficial than cooperation for university students? Give reasons and examples to support your opinion.","cn":"讨论竞争与合作哪一个对大学生成长更有利：有人看重竞争提升学习效率，有人强调合作；表明立场并说明理由。","requirements":["120–180 words","state your opinion","give reasons","use examples"],"outline":["引入：绩点、竞赛、社团，竞争与合作同时存在于校园。","主体：竞争让人看见差距、逼出潜力；合作让人学会倾听与分工，两者并非对立。","结尾：我的立场——以合作为底色，让竞争成为向前的推力。"],"model":"On campus, competition and cooperation appear to pull in opposite directions. Some students believe that competing for grades and prizes pushes them to study harder. Others hold that learning to work in collaboration with others matters far more. I agree that competition can be useful. A well-run contest shows us where we stand and forces us out of comfortable habits. Yet competition alone easily becomes exhausting, for it teaches us to watch others rather than the subject itself. Cooperation, by contrast, asks us to explain our ideas aloud, to listen, and to divide work fairly. These are the skills we will need long after the exam is forgotten. In the long run, I would choose cooperation as the foundation and let competition serve as a push. When a team shares its notes and questions, the whole group moves forward, and the quiet student who feared speaking begins to take part.","phrases":[{"en":"compete with sb. for sth.","zh":"与某人竞争某物"},{"en":"work in collaboration with","zh":"与……合作"},{"en":"team spirit","zh":"团队精神"},{"en":"in the long run","zh":"从长远看"},{"en":"be conducive to","zh":"有助于"},{"en":"to sum up","zh":"总而言之"}],"source":"写作练习材料《Writing Topics for practice(1).docx》（北师大珠海大一英语课程，真实题干）"},{"id":"write-value-of-solitude","level":"cet4","title":"On the Value of Solitude","topic":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on the value of solitude. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.","cn":"以「独处的价值」为题写一篇短文：说明独处不是孤僻，而是让人听见内心、恢复专注与创造力的时刻。","requirements":["120–180 words","state your view on solitude","give reasons or examples","avoid empty slogans"],"outline":["引入：安静的时刻并非空虚，而是自我对话的开始。","主体：独处如何帮助大学生整理情绪、深化思考，可举散步或阅读的例子。","结尾：学会与自己相处，才能更从容地与世界相处。"],"model":"Many students fear being alone. They fill every empty hour with music, messages, or noise, as if silence were a sign that something is wrong. I have come to see solitude differently. Far from being lonely, a quiet hour is a chance to listen to one's inner voice. When I walk by the lake after class without my phone, the questions I have been avoiding slowly surface: what I actually want to learn, why a certain class leaves me tired. In the quiet hours I can think a thought to its end instead of stopping halfway. Reading alone works the same way; a book becomes a conversation rather than a background sound. Solitude is not a withdrawal from the world. It is a short pause that lets us return with a sense of clarity. To come to terms with oneself is, I believe, the beginning of any honest relationship with other people.","phrases":[{"en":"in the quiet hours","zh":"在安静的时刻"},{"en":"listen to one's inner voice","zh":"倾听内心的声音"},{"en":"recharge one's mind","zh":"让头脑恢复元气"},{"en":"far from being lonely","zh":"远非孤独"},{"en":"a sense of clarity","zh":"一种澄明感"},{"en":"come to terms with oneself","zh":"与自己和解"}],"source":"原创（仿四级风格）"},{"id":"write-books-that-shape-us","level":"cet4","title":"The Books That Shape Us","topic":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on the book that has shaped you most. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.","cn":"以「塑造我的书」为题：讲述一本书如何改变你看世界的方式，写具体的影响与例证。","requirements":["120–180 words","name one book","explain its influence","give a concrete example"],"outline":["引入：有些书读完就忘，有些书却在多年后仍在说话。","主体：那本书讲了什么，哪个细节击中了我，它怎样改变了我的选择或看法。","结尾：书不会替我们生活，但它给了我们重新看待生活的角度。"],"model":"Not every book stays with us. Some are read quickly and forgotten, while a few seem to keep speaking years later. For me, that book is Hesse's Siddhartha. I first read it in my last year of high school, when I was anxious about which university to choose. What touched me was not its plot but a quiet scene in which Siddhartha sits by a river and learns to listen. It made me realize that a decision is not only a calculation of advantages; it can also be a way of becoming who we are. In retrospect, that scene gave me the courage to choose a major my parents doubted, and I stopped turning to others for approval. Books do not live our lives for us. They hand us a different pair of eyes, and we do the walking.","phrases":[{"en":"leave a deep impression on","zh":"给……留下深刻印象"},{"en":"see the world through different eyes","zh":"以不同的眼光看世界"},{"en":"turn to ... for comfort","zh":"向……寻求慰藉"},{"en":"a turning point","zh":"转折点"},{"en":"in retrospect","zh":"回想起来"},{"en":"resonate with","zh":"与……产生共鸣"}],"source":"原创（仿四级风格）"},{"id":"write-museums","level":"cet4","title":"The Value of Visiting Museums","topic":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on the value of visiting museums. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.","cn":"以「参观博物馆的意义」为题：在数字时代，实体博物馆为何仍值得一去；用具体展品说明它带来的在场感与专注。","requirements":["120–180 words","state your view on museums","give reasons or examples","avoid empty slogans"],"outline":["引入：数字时代似乎什么都能搜到，博物馆是否多余？","主体：实物带来的年代感与在场感；看展迫使你慢下来，这种专注在日常生活中稀缺。","结尾：博物馆保存的不是死物，而是一座城市的记忆与注意力。"],"model":"Some people wonder why we still need museums in an age when almost anything can be seen online. A visit to a museum, however, offers something a screen cannot. Standing in front of a real object, we sense its age and its weight. In a history museum I once saw a simple clay bowl that a child of my age had used four thousand years ago. The online photograph had told me the same facts, but the object itself made the past feel close. Museums also teach us to slow down. We cannot click to the next room; we walk, stop, and look. That patience is rare in daily life. Museums are therefore not storehouses of dead things. They are places where memory is kept alive and where we practise attention. A city without them would be poorer in spirit, no matter how fast its network is.","phrases":[{"en":"far from being useless","zh":"远非无用"},{"en":"a sense of presence","zh":"在场感"},{"en":"bring the past to life","zh":"让过去鲜活起来"},{"en":"slow down and look","zh":"放慢脚步去看"},{"en":"keep ... alive","zh":"使……得以延续"},{"en":"no matter how","zh":"无论多么……"}],"source":"原创（仿四级风格）"},{"id":"write-curiosity","level":"cet4","title":"On Keeping Curiosity","topic":"Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay on the importance of keeping curiosity in learning. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.","cn":"以「保持好奇」为题：借 Perkin 意外发现紫色染料的故事，谈好奇心如何让学习保持活力，并给出自己的例子。","requirements":["120–180 words","state your view on curiosity","support with an example","avoid empty slogans"],"outline":["引入：Perkin 没有丢掉失败的实验，才有了紫色染料——好奇心的力量。","主体：好奇心始于“为什么”；考试压力下很多人不再发问，但小小的偏离反而让知识留在心里。","结尾：机会偏爱有准备的头脑，保持好奇就是保持准备。"],"model":"Perkin's purple dye was discovered because a young chemist refused to throw away an unexpected result. His story suggests that curiosity is not a luxury for scientists but a habit worth keeping. Curiosity begins with a simple question: why is this so? Children ask it constantly, yet many of us stop asking once exams begin. I have found that the habit can survive. Last term, a strange sentence in a reading passage made me spend an afternoon in the library. The answer was not on the exam, but I still remember it today. Such small detours keep our minds awake. They also make us better students, because a question we choose ourselves is easier to carry than one we are given. Chance, as Pasteur said, favours the prepared mind. To stay curious is to keep preparing.","phrases":[{"en":"refuse to do sth.","zh":"拒绝做某事"},{"en":"a habit worth keeping","zh":"值得保持的习惯"},{"en":"a small detour from the syllabus","zh":"课程之外的一点小小偏离"},{"en":"keep one's mind awake","zh":"让头脑保持清醒"},{"en":"favour the prepared mind","zh":"偏爱有准备的头脑"},{"en":"stay curious","zh":"保持好奇"}],"source":"原创（仿四级风格）"}],"listening":[{"id":"listen-play-1","level":"cet4","title":"A Short Conversation about Children and Play","source":{"textId":"u2c","unit":"u2","label":"游戏 Unit 2 · Text C · The Power of Play"},"script":[{"speaker":"M","line":"Hi, Lisa. Did you go to the talk on children and play yesterday?"},{"speaker":"W","line":"Yes, I did. I almost missed it because I was finishing a paper."},{"speaker":"M","line":"What did the speaker say? I heard it was about how children learn."},{"speaker":"W","line":"She said play is not just fun. It helps children control their feelings and solve problems."},{"speaker":"M","line":"That sounds important. I always thought play was simply a way to pass the time."},{"speaker":"W","line":"Many people think so. But the speaker said schools are cutting play time."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Why? That seems strange."},{"speaker":"W","line":"Because of pressure to raise test scores. Teachers want children to prepare for exams."},{"speaker":"M","line":"So children play less than before?"},{"speaker":"W","line":"Yes. In some studies, children play about eight hours less each week than they did twenty years ago."},{"speaker":"M","line":"That is a big change. Did she offer any advice?"},{"speaker":"W","line":"She said parents and teachers can join children's play and ask questions. That keeps learning fun."},{"speaker":"M","line":"I would like to hear that talk myself."},{"speaker":"W","line":"I recorded part of it. I can share it with you tonight."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Thanks. That would help my paper too."},{"speaker":"W","line":"Let us meet in the library after dinner."}],"questions":[{"no":1,"stem":"What are the two speakers mainly talking about?","options":["A. A lecture on the importance of children's play.","B. A paper the woman is writing.","C. How to prepare for final exams.","D. A new library schedule."],"answer":0,"explain":"主旨题。对话开头男士问“Did you go to the talk on children and play yesterday?”，随后两人围绕讲座内容展开，A 项正确。B 项只是女士提及的附带信息；C、D 项是后文细节，不是话题中心。"},{"no":2,"stem":"According to the woman, why are schools reducing play time?","options":["A. Children prefer exams to games.","B. There is pressure to raise test scores.","C. Schools lack playgrounds.","D. There are not enough teachers."],"answer":1,"explain":"细节题。女士说“Because of pressure to raise test scores. Teachers want children to prepare for exams.”，B 项为原文同义复现。A、C、D 项对话中没有提到。"},{"no":3,"stem":"What will the woman do tonight?","options":["A. Attend another lecture.","B. Share a recording with the man.","C. Finish her paper alone.","D. Visit a primary school."],"answer":1,"explain":"细节题。结尾女士说“I recorded part of it. I can share it with you tonight.”，B 项正确。A、C、D 项均与结尾信息不符。"}]},{"id":"listen-hotel-1","level":"cet4","title":"A Long Conversation about a Hotel Internship","source":{"textId":"u3b","unit":"u3","label":"动机 Unit 3 · Text B · Motivating Employees under Adverse Conditions"},"script":[{"speaker":"W","line":"Good morning, Professor Lee. Do you have a minute? I want to ask about the internship at the hotel."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Of course, Anna. Sit down. What would you like to know?"},{"speaker":"W","line":"I was offered a place in the front office team, but some friends say hotel work is boring and badly paid."},{"speaker":"M","line":"That depends on how the job is managed. In my research, motivation matters more than pay for many young staff."},{"speaker":"W","line":"What do you mean?"},{"speaker":"M","line":"If workers feel their ideas are heard, they stay longer and work harder. If managers only give orders, people lose interest."},{"speaker":"W","line":"So the manager matters a lot."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Yes. Good managers explain why a task is important. They also give staff a chance to learn new skills."},{"speaker":"W","line":"That makes sense. But what about the long hours? I am worried I will have no time to study."},{"speaker":"M","line":"You should ask about the schedule before you accept. Some hotels offer flexible hours for students."},{"speaker":"W","line":"I did not know that. Should I mention my courses in the interview?"},{"speaker":"M","line":"Certainly. Tell them when you are free, and ask whether training is provided."},{"speaker":"W","line":"What if they say no?"},{"speaker":"M","line":"Then you can decide whether the experience is still worth it. The first job is not the last one."},{"speaker":"W","line":"Thank you. I feel much clearer now."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Good. Send me an email after the interview and tell me how it goes."}],"questions":[{"no":1,"stem":"What is the woman mainly asking about?","options":["A. Whether to accept a hotel internship.","B. How to write a research paper.","C. Why hotel food is expensive.","D. How to apply for a scholarship."],"answer":0,"explain":"主旨题。女士开场就说想询问酒店实习岗位的事，整段对话围绕是否接受、如何面试展开，A 项正确。B 项只提到她关心学习时间，不是主题。"},{"no":2,"stem":"According to the professor, what matters most for young hotel staff?","options":["A. High pay.","B. Feeling that their ideas are heard.","C. Short working hours.","D. Free meals."],"answer":1,"explain":"细节题。教授说“motivation matters more than pay”并解释“If workers feel their ideas are heard, they stay longer and work harder”，B 项正确。A 项与教授观点相反；C、D 项未提及。"},{"no":3,"stem":"What does the professor advise the woman to do at the interview?","options":["A. Ask about flexible hours and training.","B. Refuse to work at weekends.","C. Talk about her hobbies only.","D. Bring a friend with her."],"answer":0,"explain":"细节题。教授建议她“ask about the schedule”“ask whether training is provided”，并说明可以说明哪些时间有空，A 项概括准确。B、C、D 项均无依据。"}]},{"id":"listen-glass-1","level":"cet4","title":"A Short Talk on the History of Glass","source":{"textId":"u4b","unit":"u4","label":"历史 Unit 4 · Text B · The History of Glass"},"script":[{"speaker":"M","line":"Today I want to talk about the history of glass."},{"speaker":"M","line":"People have used glass for thousands of years."},{"speaker":"M","line":"The earliest natural glass was formed by volcanoes."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Ancient people used it to make sharp tips for spears."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Man-made glass appeared much later."},{"speaker":"M","line":"By 4000 BC, people were using glass to coat stone beads."},{"speaker":"M","line":"The first hollow glass container was made around 1500 BC."},{"speaker":"M","line":"It was produced by covering a sand core with melted glass."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Glass blowing became the most common method from the first century BC."},{"speaker":"M","line":"At first, glass was often coloured because the raw materials were not pure."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Later, Roman craftsmen learned to make colourless glass."},{"speaker":"M","line":"The Romans kept their skills secret."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Only after their empire fell did the knowledge spread across Europe."},{"speaker":"M","line":"In the tenth century, craftsmen in Venice became famous for their skill."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Then, in the seventeenth century, George Ravenscroft invented lead crystal glass."},{"speaker":"M","line":"It was softer, easier to decorate, and more brilliant."},{"speaker":"M","line":"This invention made lenses and telescopes possible."},{"speaker":"M","line":"In the nineteenth century, glass began to be used in large buildings."},{"speaker":"M","line":"The Crystal Palace in London showed how glass could change architecture."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Today glass is used in homes, cars, and medicine."},{"speaker":"M","line":"It is also recycled, which saves energy and protects the environment."}],"questions":[{"no":1,"stem":"What is the speaker mainly talking about?","options":["A. The history and development of glass.","B. How to recycle glass bottles.","C. The life of George Ravenscroft.","D. Architecture in London."],"answer":0,"explain":"主旨题。讲稿从天然玻璃、最早的玻璃容器一路讲到现代玻璃与回收，A 项概括最完整。B、C 项只是末尾或中段的细节；D 项只是水晶宫一例。"},{"no":2,"stem":"What was special about Ravenscroft's glass?","options":["A. It was cheaper than Roman glass.","B. It was more brilliant and easier to decorate.","C. It was made from volcanic rock.","D. It could not be coloured."],"answer":1,"explain":"细节题。原文说“The new glass he created was softer and easier to decorate, and had a higher refractive index, adding to its brilliance and beauty”，B 项与之对应。A、C、D 项均无依据。"},{"no":3,"stem":"What does the speaker say about glass today?","options":["A. It is rarely used in buildings.","B. It is recycled to save energy.","C. It is no longer made in factories.","D. It is too expensive for most people."],"answer":1,"explain":"细节题。结尾说“It is also recycled, which saves energy and protects the environment”，B 项正确。A 项与“homes, cars, and medicine”矛盾；C、D 项未提及。"}]},{"id":"listen-ants-1","level":"cet4","title":"A Short Talk on the Intelligence of Ants","source":{"textId":"u5c","unit":"u5","label":"智力 Unit 5 · Text C · Ant Intelligence"},"script":[{"speaker":"M","line":"Most people think of monkeys and apes when they hear the word intelligence."},{"speaker":"M","line":"But some insects lead social lives that are surprisingly complex."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Ants, for example, store food and defend their nests."},{"speaker":"M","line":"They also send chemical signals to warn each other of danger."},{"speaker":"M","line":"The scientist Lewis Thomas once wrote that ants are like humans in many ways."},{"speaker":"M","line":"They farm fungi and keep aphids as livestock."},{"speaker":"M","line":"They even send armies to war."},{"speaker":"M","line":"However, ants do not pass knowledge from one generation to the next."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Everything they can do is written in their genes."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Human children, by contrast, learn most skills from other people."},{"speaker":"M","line":"At first, this seems to give humans a great advantage."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Yet ant farming is at least sustainable."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Ants do not ruin their environment."},{"speaker":"M","line":"They have been farmers for fifty million years, far longer than humans."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Some ant colonies are huge."},{"speaker":"M","line":"One supercolony in Japan had millions of workers and a million queens."},{"speaker":"M","line":"Now researchers have found that desert ants can find their way home."},{"speaker":"M","line":"They remember distances and directions as they travel."},{"speaker":"M","line":"They can even pass complex messages about food to other ants."},{"speaker":"M","line":"So the next time you see an ant in your kitchen, you may want to watch it for a while."}],"questions":[{"no":1,"stem":"What is the speaker mainly discussing?","options":["A. Why ants are pests in kitchens.","B. Evidence that ants show a kind of intelligence.","C. How monkeys learn from humans.","D. The diet of desert animals."],"answer":1,"explain":"主旨题。讲稿用化学信号、养殖真菌、导航与传递信息等例子说明蚂蚁具有某种智能，B 项正确。A 项是结尾的俏皮话；C、D 项只是零散提及。"},{"no":2,"stem":"According to the speaker, how are ants different from humans?","options":["A. Ants cannot communicate.","B. Ants do not pass knowledge through culture.","C. Ants live alone.","D. Ants do not need food."],"answer":1,"explain":"细节题。原文说“ants do not pass knowledge from one generation to the next. Everything they can do is written in their genes”，而人类“learn most skills from other people”，B 项正确。A 项与“send chemical signals”矛盾；C、D 项明显错误。"},{"no":3,"stem":"What have researchers found about desert ants?","options":["A. They can remember their way home.","B. They cannot find food at night.","C. They live for fifty years.","D. They always work alone."],"answer":0,"explain":"细节题。原文说 desert ants“find their way home”“remember distances and directions”，A 项正确。B、C、D 项均无依据，C 项混淆了“fifty million years”。"}]}],"cloze3":[{"id":"c3-01","unit":"u1","source":"课本 Unit 1 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"As a boy, Perkin’s ___ prompted early interests in the arts, sciences, photography, and engineering.","options":["curiosity","courage","caution"],"answer":0,"explain":"curiosity 好奇心；prompt early interests 是课文搭配，courage/caution 与“激发兴趣”不搭。"},{"id":"c3-02","unit":"u1","source":"课本 Unit 1 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Not long after that, Perkin made the scientific ___ that would bring him both fame and fortune.","options":["breakthrough","breakdown","outbreak"],"answer":0,"explain":"scientific breakthrough 科学突破；breakdown 故障、outbreak 爆发均不能与 scientific 搭配。"},{"id":"c3-03","unit":"u1","source":"课本 Unit 1 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"During his vacation in 1856, Perkin spent his time in the ___ on the top floor of his family’s house.","options":["laboratory","library","lavatory"],"answer":0,"explain":"in the laboratory 在实验室；与后文“顶层做实验”一致，library/lavatory 语义不合。"},{"id":"c3-04","unit":"u1","source":"课本 Unit 1 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Perkin quickly grasped that his purple solution could be used to colour fabric, thus making it the world’s first synthetic ___.","options":["dye","drug","dose"],"answer":0,"explain":"synthetic dye 合成染料；上文的 colour fabric 已限定语义，drug/dose 指药物剂量。"},{"id":"c3-05","unit":"u1","source":"课本 Unit 1 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Those speeches fired the young ___’s enthusiasm further, and he later went on to attend the Royal College of Chemistry, which he succeeded in entering in 1853, at the age of 15.","options":["chemist","chemistry","chemical"],"answer":0,"explain":"the young chemist's 需要指人的名词所有格；chemistry 学科、chemical 化学品均不能加 's 指人。"},{"id":"c3-06","unit":"u2","source":"课本 Unit 2 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Not only is more of the brain involved in play than was suspected, but it also seems to activate higher ___ processes.","options":["cognitive","creative","cultural"],"answer":0,"explain":"higher cognitive processes 更高认知过程；与“大脑参与”呼应，creative/cultural 不修饰 processes 的学术义。"},{"id":"c3-07","unit":"u2","source":"课本 Unit 2 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"The latest idea suggests that play has ___ to build big brains.","options":["evolved","involved","revolved"],"answer":0,"explain":"has evolved to build 进化出……的功能；involve/revolve 与 play 的进化语境不符。"},{"id":"c3-08","unit":"u2","source":"课本 Unit 2 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Playfulness, it seems, is common only among mammals, although a few of the larger-brained birds also ___.","options":["indulge","include","invade"],"answer":0,"explain":"indulge 此处作不及物动词“尽情享受”；include/invade 需要宾语，语法与语义都不合。"},{"id":"c3-09","unit":"u2","source":"课本 Unit 2 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"But if play is not simply a ___ hiccup, as biologists once thought, why did it evolve?","options":["developmental","development","developed"],"answer":0,"explain":"a developmental hiccup 发育中的小插曲；需形容词修饰 hiccup，development/developed 词性不符。"},{"id":"c3-10","unit":"u2","source":"课本 Unit 2 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Animals at play often use unique signs—tail-wagging in dogs, for example—to indicate that activity ___ resembling adult behaviour is not really in earnest.","options":["superficially","superstitiously","supremely"],"answer":0,"explain":"superficially resembling 表面上像；superstitiously 迷信地、supremely 极其，均不符“表面相似”的语义。"},{"id":"c3-11","unit":"u3","source":"课本 Unit 3 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"In addition, or maybe as a result, high employee ___ has been a recurring problem throughout the hospitality industry.","options":["turnover","turnout","turnaround"],"answer":0,"explain":"employee turnover 员工流失率；turnout 出席人数、turnaround 好转/周转，均不指员工流动。"},{"id":"c3-12","unit":"u3","source":"课本 Unit 3 · Text B 原句挖空","stem":"The way rewards are distributed should be ___ so that employees perceive that rewards or outcomes are equitable and equal to the inputs given.","options":["transparent","transitional","transient"],"answer":0,"explain":"should be transparent 应当透明；transitional 过渡的、transient 短暂的，语义不符。"},{"id":"c3-13","unit":"u3","source":"课本 Unit 3 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"When these factors are unfavorable, job ___ may result.","options":["dissatisfaction","distribution","disagreement"],"answer":0,"explain":"job dissatisfaction 工作不满；distribution 分配、disagreement 分歧，均不能与 job 搭配。"},{"id":"c3-14","unit":"u3","source":"课本 Unit 3 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Ng and Sorensen (2008) demonstrated that when managers provide ___ to employees, motivate employees to work together, and remove obstacles preventing effective performance, employees feel more obligated to stay with the company.","options":["recognition","recruitment","reputation"],"answer":0,"explain":"provide recognition to employees 给予认可；recruitment 招聘、reputation 名声与“管理者给予”语义不合。"},{"id":"c3-15","unit":"u3","source":"课本 Unit 3 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Despite this recognition of the importance of employee development, the hospitality industry has historically been ___ by underdeveloped HR practices (Lucas, 2002).","options":["dominated","donated","denominated"],"answer":0,"explain":"has been dominated by 一直由……主导；donate 捐赠、denominate 命名，均不合语境。"},{"id":"c3-16","unit":"u4","source":"课本 Unit 4 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"The conviction that historical relics provide infallible ___ about the past is rooted in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, when science was regarded as objective and value free.","options":["testimony","testimonial","ceremony"],"answer":0,"explain":"provide infallible testimony about the past 提供可靠证词；testimonial 推荐书、ceremony 仪式均不合。"},{"id":"c3-17","unit":"u4","source":"课本 Unit 4 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Human bias is ___, but another source of bias in the representation of history has to do with the transitory nature of the materials themselves.","options":["inevitable","invisible","invaluable"],"answer":0,"explain":"Human bias is inevitable 人的偏见不可避免；invisible 看不见的、invaluable 无价的，语义不合。"},{"id":"c3-18","unit":"u4","source":"课本 Unit 4 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"Such developments have been criticised as an intolerable ___, but the success of many historical theme parks and similar locations suggests that the majority of the public does not share this opinion.","options":["vulgarisation","variation","valuation"],"answer":0,"explain":"an intolerable vulgarisation 难以容忍的庸俗化；variation 变化、valuation 估价均不合。"},{"id":"c3-19","unit":"u4","source":"课本 Unit 4 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"For example, museums have adopted story lines for exhibitions, sites have accepted ‘theming’ as a relevant tool, and theme parks are moving towards more ___ and research-based presentations.","options":["authenticity","authority","austerity"],"answer":0,"explain":"more authenticity 更真实；authority 权威、austerity 紧缩，均与“主题公园更接近真实”不符。"},{"id":"c3-20","unit":"u4","source":"课本 Unit 4 · Text A 原句挖空","stem":"No wonder that people who stroll around exhibitions are filled with ___; the evidence in museums indicates that life was so much better in the past.","options":["nostalgia","nausea","notion"],"answer":0,"explain":"be filled with nostalgia 满怀乡愁；与后文“过去生活更好”呼应，nausea 恶心、notion 观念不合。"}],"match15":[{"id":"m15-u1","title":"Unit 1 · Biography","source":"课本 Unit 1 Text A/B/C 原句挖空 + 词表匹配（一词一句）","words":["curiosity","enthusiasm","laboratory","breakthrough","synthetic","dye","fortune","chemist","aniline","quinine","malaria","lectures","discovery","chemistry","mauve"],"items":[{"no":1,"stem":"As a boy, Perkin’s ___ prompted early interests in the arts, sciences, photography, and engineering.","answer":"curiosity"},{"no":2,"stem":"But it was a chance stumbling upon a run-down, yet functional, laboratory in his late grandfather’s home that solidified the young man’s ___ for chemistry.","answer":"enthusiasm"},{"no":3,"stem":"During his vacation in 1856, Perkin spent his time in the ___ on the top floor of his family’s house.","answer":"laboratory"},{"no":4,"stem":"Not long after that, Perkin made the scientific ___ that would bring him both fame and fortune.","answer":"breakthrough"},{"no":5,"stem":"Thus, when Hofmann made some passing comments about the desirability of a ___ substitute for quinine, it was unsurprising that his star pupil was moved to take up the challenge.","answer":"synthetic"},{"no":6,"stem":"Perkin quickly grasped that his purple solution could be used to colour fabric, thus making it the world’s first synthetic ___.","answer":"dye"},{"no":7,"stem":"Although Perkin’s fame was achieved and ___ assured by his first discovery, the chemist continued his research.","answer":"fortune"},{"no":8,"stem":"Those speeches fired the young ___’s enthusiasm further, and he later went on to attend the Royal College of Chemistry, which he succeeded in entering in 1853, at the age of 15.","answer":"chemist"},{"no":9,"stem":"He was attempting to manufacture quinine from ___, an inexpensive and readily available coal tar waste product.","answer":"aniline"},{"no":10,"stem":"At the time, ___ was the only viable medical treatment for malaria.","answer":"quinine"},{"no":11,"stem":"And, in what would have been particularly pleasing to Perkin, their current use is in the search for a vaccine against ___.","answer":"malaria"},{"no":12,"stem":"His talent and devotion to the subject were perceived by his teacher, Thomas Hall, who encouraged him to attend a series of ___ given by the eminent scientist Michael Faraday at the Royal Institution.","answer":"lectures"},{"no":13,"stem":"It was against this backdrop that Perkin’s ___ was made.","answer":"discovery"},{"no":14,"stem":"As a student at the City of London School, Perkin became immersed in the study of ___.","answer":"chemistry"},{"no":15,"stem":"Perkin originally named his dye Tyrian Purple, but it later became commonly known as ___ (from the French for the plant used to make the colour violet).","answer":"mauve"}],"explain":"每句都是课文原句挖去一词；15 个词与 15 句一一对应，答案唯一。"},{"id":"m15-u2","title":"Unit 2 · Play","source":"课本 Unit 2 Text A/B/C 原句挖空 + 词表匹配（一词一句）","words":["play","learning","dichotomy","regulate","cognitive","species","spontaneous","emotional","mammals","energy","predatory","kittens","behaviour","brains","endurance"],"items":[{"no":1,"stem":"Does ___ help develop bigger, better brains?","answer":"play"},{"no":2,"stem":"‘I concluded it’s to do with ___, and with the importance of environmental data to the brain during development,’ he says.","answer":"learning"},{"no":3,"stem":"Our society has created a false ___ between play and learning.","answer":"dichotomy"},{"no":4,"stem":"When she bosses him around as his teacher, she’s practising how to ___ her emotions through pretence.","answer":"regulate"},{"no":5,"stem":"Not only is more of the brain involved in play than was suspected, but it also seems to activate higher ___ processes.","answer":"cognitive"},{"no":6,"stem":"‘If the function of play was to get into shape,’ says Byers, ‘the optimum time for playing would depend on when it was most advantageous for the young of a particular ___ to do so.","answer":"species"},{"no":7,"stem":"‘The type of play we are interested in is child-initiated, ___ and unpredictable—but as soon as you ask a five-year-old “to play”, then you as the researcher have intervened,’ explains Dr. Sara Baker.","answer":"spontaneous"},{"no":8,"stem":"Gibson adds: ‘Playful behaviour is also an important indicator of healthy social and ___ development.","answer":"emotional"},{"no":9,"stem":"Playfulness, it seems, is common only among ___, although a few of the larger-brained birds also indulge.","answer":"mammals"},{"no":10,"stem":"It is also extremely expensive in terms of ___.","answer":"energy"},{"no":11,"stem":"In one study, behavioural ecologist Tim Caro, from the University of California, looked at the ___ play of kittens and their predatory behaviour when they reached adulthood.","answer":"predatory"},{"no":12,"stem":"Children engrossed in a make-believe world, fox cubs play-fighting or ___ teasing a ball of string aren’t just having fun.","answer":"kittens"},{"no":13,"stem":"Animals at play often use unique signs—tail-wagging in dogs, for example—to indicate that activity superficially resembling adult ___ is not really in earnest.","answer":"behaviour"},{"no":14,"stem":"The latest idea suggests that play has evolved to build big ___.","answer":"brains"},{"no":15,"stem":"Another has been that it allows young animals to get in shape for adult life by improving their respiratory ___.","answer":"endurance"}],"explain":"每句都是课文原句挖去一词；15 个词与 15 句一一对应，答案唯一。"},{"id":"m15-u3","title":"Unit 3 · Motivation","source":"课本 Unit 3 Text A/B/C 原句挖空 + 词表匹配（一词一句）","words":["motivation","hospitality","turnover","transparent","dissatisfaction","recognition","achievement","performance","challenge","productivity","autonomy","rewards","management","employees","salary"],"items":[{"no":1,"stem":"Herzberg (1966) proposes that people have two major types of needs, the first being extrinsic ___ factors relating to the context in which work is performed, rather than the work itself.","answer":"motivation"},{"no":2,"stem":"Despite this recognition of the importance of employee development, the ___ industry has historically been dominated by underdeveloped HR practices (Lucas, 2002).","answer":"hospitality"},{"no":3,"stem":"In addition, or maybe as a result, high employee ___ has been a recurring problem throughout the hospitality industry.","answer":"turnover"},{"no":4,"stem":"The way rewards are distributed should be ___ so that employees perceive that rewards or outcomes are equitable and equal to the inputs given.","answer":"transparent"},{"no":5,"stem":"When these factors are unfavorable, job ___ may result.","answer":"dissatisfaction"},{"no":6,"stem":"Ng and Sorensen (2008) demonstrated that when managers provide ___ to employees, motivate employees to work together, and remove obstacles preventing effective performance, employees feel more obligated to stay with the company.","answer":"recognition"},{"no":7,"stem":"Employees also have intrinsic motivation needs or motivators, which include such factors as ___ and recognition.","answer":"achievement"},{"no":8,"stem":"A critical ingredient in the success of hotels is developing and maintaining superior ___ from their employees.","answer":"performance"},{"no":9,"stem":"The ___ to management is how to motivate employees under such retrenchment conditions.","answer":"challenge"},{"no":10,"stem":"___ often suffers, as employees spend their time sharing rumours and providing one another with moral support rather than focusing on their jobs.","answer":"productivity"},{"no":11,"stem":"Some of the more obvious rewards that managers allocate include pay, promotions, ___, job scope and depth, and the opportunity to participate in goal-setting and decision-making.","answer":"autonomy"},{"no":12,"stem":"Managers could use their knowledge of each employee to personalise the ___ over which they have control.","answer":"rewards"},{"no":13,"stem":"What Human Resource ___ (HRM) practices should organizations invest in to acquire and retain great employees.","answer":"management"},{"no":14,"stem":"Some hotels aim to provide superior working conditions for their ___.","answer":"employees"},{"no":15,"stem":"There is a strong link between the perceptions of employees and particular factors of their work environment that are separate from the work itself, including company policies, ___ and vacations.","answer":"salary"}],"explain":"每句都是课文原句挖去一词；15 个词与 15 句一一对应，答案唯一。"},{"id":"m15-u4","title":"Unit 4 · History","source":"课本 Unit 4 Text A/B/C 原句挖空 + 词表匹配（一词一句）","words":["heritage","relics","bias","vulgarisation","authenticity","evidence","transitory","contemporary","museum","ancestors","interpretation","sites","visitors","scholars","exhibits"],"items":[{"no":1,"stem":"The key word in ___ display is now ‘experience’, the more exciting the better and, if possible, involving all the senses.","answer":"heritage"},{"no":2,"stem":"The conviction that historical ___ provide infallible testimony about the past is rooted in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, when science was regarded as objective and value free.","answer":"relics"},{"no":3,"stem":"And no matter how exciting the result, it would contain a lot more ___ than the presentations provided by experts.","answer":"bias"},{"no":4,"stem":"Such developments have been criticised as an intolerable ___, but the success of many historical theme parks and similar locations suggests that the majority of the public does not share this opinion.","answer":"vulgarisation"},{"no":5,"stem":"For example, museums have adopted story lines for exhibitions, sites have accepted ‘theming’ as a relevant tool, and theme parks are moving towards more ___ and research-based presentations.","answer":"authenticity"},{"no":6,"stem":"No wonder that people who stroll around exhibitions are filled with nostalgia; the ___ in museums indicates that life was so much better in the past.","answer":"evidence"},{"no":7,"stem":"Human bias is inevitable, but another source of bias in the representation of history has to do with the ___ nature of the materials themselves.","answer":"transitory"},{"no":8,"stem":"Such presentations tell us more about ___ perceptions of the world than about our ancestors.","answer":"contemporary"},{"no":9,"stem":"As one writer observes: ‘Although it is now evident that artefacts are as easily altered as chronicles, public faith in their veracity endures: a tangible relic seems ipso facto real.’ Such conviction was, until recently, reflected in ___ displays.","answer":"museum"},{"no":10,"stem":"Whales (including the small whales we call dolphins) and dugongs, with their close cousins the manatees, ceased to be land creatures altogether and reverted to the full marine habits of their remote ___.","answer":"ancestors"},{"no":11,"stem":"There is one compensation, however, for the professionals who make these interpretations: if they did not provide the ___, visitors would do it for themselves, based on their own ideas, misconceptions and prejudices.","answer":"interpretation"},{"no":12,"stem":"In a related development, the sharp distinction between museum and heritage ___ on the one hand, and theme parks on the other, is gradually evaporating.","answer":"sites"},{"no":13,"stem":"Heritage and museum experts do not have to invent stories and recreate historical environments to attract their ___: their assets are already in place.","answer":"visitors"},{"no":14,"stem":"Museums used to look—and some still do—much like storage rooms of objects packed together in showcases: good for ___ who wanted to study the subtle differences in design, but not for the ordinary visitor, to whom it all looked alike.","answer":"scholars"},{"no":15,"stem":"However, ___ must be both based on artefacts and facts as we know them, and attractively presented.","answer":"exhibits"}],"explain":"每句都是课文原句挖去一词；15 个词与 15 句一一对应，答案唯一。"}],"translation_u14a":[{"id":"tr-u1-01","unit":"u1","title":"U1 · 珀金的偶然发现（强调句 it was ... that）","zh":"但正是在祖父家中偶然发现一个破旧却能运转的实验室，让这个年轻人对化学的热情更加坚定。","reference":"But it was a chance stumbling upon a run-down, yet functional, laboratory in his late grandfather's home that solidified the young man's enthusiasm for chemistry.","source":"第四周/R&W Translation.pptx · 例句 1（课件原句+译文）"},{"id":"tr-u1-02","unit":"u1","title":"U1 · 老师发现天赋（被动→主动）","zh":"他的老师托马斯·霍尔发现了他在这一学科上的天赋与奉献精神，并鼓励他去参加著名科学家迈克尔·法拉第在皇家学院举办的一系列讲座。","reference":"His talent and devotion to the subject were perceived by his teacher, Thomas Hall, who encouraged him to attend a series of lectures given by the eminent scientist Michael Faraday at the Royal Institution.","source":"第四周/R&W Translation.pptx · 例句 2（课件原句+译文）"},{"id":"tr-u1-03","unit":"u1","title":"U1 · 科学突破（定语从句一刀两断）","zh":"不久之后，珀金取得了科学突破，这将为他带来名望和财富。","reference":"Not long after that, Perkin made the scientific breakthrough that would bring him both fame and fortune.","source":"第四周/R&W Translation.pptx · 例句 3（课件原句+译文）"},{"id":"tr-u1-04","unit":"u1","title":"U1 · 强调句 + 被动（隐藏“被”）","zh":"正是在这种背景下，珀金完成了这项发现。","reference":"It was against this backdrop that Perkin's discovery was made.","source":"第四周/R&W Translation.pptx · 例句 5 / 第八周 Semester Review · S1-1（课件原句+译文）"},{"id":"tr-u2-05","unit":"u2","title":"U2 · 玩耍不只是消磨时间","zh":"玩耍也许看起来只是成年后辛勤劳作到来之前，一种无忧无虑、充满活力的消磨时光的方式，但它远不止于此。","reference":"Play may look like a carefree and exuberant way to pass the time before the hard work of adulthood comes along, but there's much more to it than that.","source":"课文 Unit 2 Text A 原句 + 课文逐句译文"},{"id":"tr-u2-06","unit":"u2","title":"U2 · 玩耍的进化目的","zh":"最新的观点认为，玩耍的进化是为了塑造更大的大脑。","reference":"The latest idea suggests that play has evolved to build big brains.","source":"课文 Unit 2 Text A 原句 + 课文逐句译文"},{"id":"tr-u3-07","unit":"u3","title":"U3 · 酒店服务与员工条件的割裂","zh":"这表明，豪华酒店为客人营造的环境与其员工的工作条件之间，一直存在着割裂。","reference":"This suggests that there has been a dichotomy between the guest environment provided in luxury hotels and the working conditions of their employees.","source":"课文 Unit 3 Text A 原句 + 课文逐句译文（课件 3-2-1 翻译法）"},{"id":"tr-u3-08","unit":"u3","title":"U3 · 人力资源管理实践","zh":"因此，酒店管理层必须发展出能够激励并留住优秀员工的人力资源实践。","reference":"It is therefore essential for hotel management to develop HRM practices that enable them to inspire and retain competent employees.","source":"课文 Unit 3 Text A 原句 + 课文逐句译文"},{"id":"tr-u3-09","unit":"u3","title":"U3 · 员工发展被忽视（让步状语）","zh":"尽管人们已认识到员工发展的重要性，酒店业在历史上却一直由不成熟的人力资源实践所主导（卢卡斯，2002）。","reference":"Despite this recognition of the importance of employee development, the hospitality industry has historically been dominated by underdeveloped HR practices (Lucas, 2002).","source":"课文 Unit 3 Text A 原句 + 课文逐句译文"},{"id":"tr-u4-10","unit":"u4","title":"U4 · 文物并非可靠证词","zh":"人们相信历史文物能为过去提供可靠无误的证词，这种信念植根于十九世纪和二十世纪初——那时科学被视为客观且价值中立的。","reference":"The conviction that historical relics provide infallible testimony about the past is rooted in the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, when science was regarded as objective and value free.","source":"课文 Unit 4 Text A 原句 + 课文逐句译文"},{"id":"tr-u4-11","unit":"u4","title":"U4 · 怀旧与博物馆叙事","zh":"难怪在展览间漫步的人会满怀乡愁；博物馆里的证据似乎在表明，过去的生活要好得多。","reference":"No wonder that people who stroll around exhibitions are filled with nostalgia; the evidence in museums indicates that life was so much better in the past.","source":"课文 Unit 4 Text A 原句 + 课文逐句译文"},{"id":"tr-u4-12","unit":"u4","title":"U4 · 呈现方式带来的偏见","zh":"这种观念，是由博物馆与遗产中心在呈现历史时的偏见所诱发的。","reference":"This notion is induced by the bias in its representation in museums and heritage centres.","source":"课文 Unit 4 Text A 原句 + 课文逐句译文"}],"paraphrase_u14a":[{"id":"pp-u1-01","unit":"u1","title":"U1 · 维多利亚女王与紫色风行","original":"Not to be outdone, England's Queen Victoria also appeared in public wearing a mauve gown, thus making it all the rage in England as well.","reference":"Soon after, Queen Victoria wore mauve, making the colour very popular in England, too.","technique":"分词结构还原为并列谓语 + 同义替换（all the rage → very popular）","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Unit 1（对应课文 u1a）"},{"id":"pp-u1-02","unit":"u1","title":"U1 · 染料鲜艳且不褪色","original":"The dye was bold and fast, and the public clamoured for more.","reference":"The colour was bright and did not fade, so people wanted more of it.","technique":"同义替换（bold → bright, fast → did not fade, clamoured for → wanted）+ 并列改因果","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Unit 1（对应课文 u1a）"},{"id":"pp-u1-03","unit":"u1","title":"U1 · 染料的现代用途（插入语重组）","original":"And, in what would have been particularly pleasing to Perkin, their current use is in the search for a vaccine against malaria.","reference":"Perkin would be happy to know that these dyes are now used to help find a malaria vaccine.","technique":"插入语改写为宾语从句 + 语态重组（被动→主动）","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Unit 1（对应课文 u1a）"},{"id":"pp-u2-04","unit":"u2","title":"U2 · 玩耍不只是“玩”","original":"Children engrossed in a make-believe world, fox cubs play-fighting or kittens teasing a ball of string aren't just having fun.","reference":"When children or young animals play, they are doing more than just enjoying themselves.","technique":"具体罗列概括为一般条件句 + 同义替换（aren't just having fun → more than enjoying themselves）","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Units 2-3（对应课文 u2a）"},{"id":"pp-u2-05","unit":"u2","title":"U2 · 玩耍激活高级认知","original":"Not only is more of the brain involved in play than was suspected, but it also seems to activate higher cognitive processes.","reference":"Play triggers advanced cognitive functions and involves more brain regions than scientists had originally believed.","technique":"倒装还原 + 主被动转换 + 同义替换（suspected → originally believed）","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Units 2-3（对应课文 u2a）"},{"id":"pp-u3-06","unit":"u3","title":"U3 · 酒店成功的关键要素","original":"A critical ingredient in the success of hotels is developing and maintaining superior performance from their employees.","reference":"For a hotel to thrive, it is essential to foster and uphold high-level performance in its staff.","technique":"名词化主语改条件句 + 同义替换（critical ingredient → essential, superior → high-level）","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Units 2-3（对应课文 u3a）"},{"id":"pp-u3-07","unit":"u3","title":"U3 · 员工流失的老问题","original":"High employee turnover has been a recurring problem throughout the hospitality industry.","reference":"The hospitality industry is well-known for its chronic issue of employees rapidly leaving and being replaced.","technique":"名词短语解释化（turnover → employees rapidly leaving and being replaced）+ 语序重组","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Units 2-3（课件练习句）"},{"id":"pp-u3-08","unit":"u3","title":"U3 · 支持员工与留任","original":"Providing support to employees gives them the confidence to perform their jobs better and the motivation to stay with the organization.","reference":"The provision of support for employees makes them confident to improve their jobs and motivates them to stay and work for the organization.","technique":"动名词主语名词化（providing → provision）+ 并列结构重组","source":"第四周/R&W Paraphrasing.pptx · Paraphrase Questions 1（课件题+参考答案）（课件练习句）"},{"id":"pp-u3-09","unit":"u3","title":"U3 · 酒店环境与员工条件的割裂","original":"This suggests that there has been a dichotomy between the guest environment provided in luxury hotels and the working conditions of their employees.","reference":"This indicates that a big gap has existed between the environment in luxury hotels for guests and the working conditions for their employees.","technique":"同义替换（dichotomy → a big gap, suggests → indicates）+ 后置定语前移","source":"第四周/R&W Paraphrasing.pptx · Paraphrase Questions 2（课件题+参考答案）（对应课文 u3a）"},{"id":"pp-u4-10","unit":"u4","title":"U4 · 主题公园与公众态度","original":"Such developments have been criticised as an intolerable vulgarisation, but the success of many historical theme parks suggests that the majority of the public does not share this opinion.","reference":"Despite being criticized as unacceptably vulgar, the widespread success of historical theme parks indicates that public opinion is very different.","technique":"让步状语改写 + 同义替换（intolerable → unacceptably, majority...not share → very different）","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Unit 4（课件练习句）"},{"id":"pp-u4-11","unit":"u4","title":"U4 · 历史阐释者的两难","original":"Those who are professionally engaged in the art of interpreting history must steer a narrow course between the demands of evidence and attractiveness.","reference":"Professional historians face a dilemma: they must navigate a narrow path between historical accuracy and public appeal.","technique":"身份名词化（those who... → professional historians）+ 抽象词具体化（evidence/attractiveness → accuracy/appeal）","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Unit 4（课件练习句）"},{"id":"pp-u4-12","unit":"u4","title":"U4 · 材料的易逝性带来偏见","original":"Human bias is inevitable, but another source of bias in the representation of history has to do with the transitory nature of the materials themselves.","reference":"While human bias is unavoidable, the fact that historical materials are impermanent and easily lost represents another significant source of bias.","technique":"让步从句重组 + 名词短语解释化（transitory nature → impermanent and easily lost）","source":"第八周/Paraphrasing_Review_12slides.pptx · Practice: Unit 4（对应课文 u4a）"}],"textbook":[{"id":"tb-u1b","unit":"u1","text":"u1b","title":"Unit 1 · Text B · Exercise 1","sourcePages":[10],"items":[{"no":1,"type":"tfng","stem":"Henderson rarely visited the area around Press estate when he was younger.","answer":"F","explain":"The passage says Alexander 'spent much of his childhood in the area' around Press Estate, playing on the beach near Eyemouth and fishing in the streams nearby - he visited often, not rarely."},{"no":2,"type":"tfng","stem":"Henderson pursued a business career because it was what his family wanted.","answer":"T","explain":"'Although he never liked the prospect of a business career, he stayed with it to please his family.'"},{"no":3,"type":"tfng","stem":"Henderson and Notman were surprised by the results of their 1865 experiment.","answer":"NG","explain":"The text only mentions that the two men 'cooperated on experiments with magnesium flares as a source of artificial light in 1865'; it says nothing about their being surprised by the results."},{"no":4,"type":"tfng","stem":"There were many similarities between Henderson’s early landscapes and those of Notman.","answer":"F","explain":"'In spite of their friendship, their styles of photography were quite different.'"},{"no":5,"type":"tfng","stem":"The studio that Henderson opened in 1866 was close to his home.","answer":"NG","explain":"The passage says he opened a photographic studio in 1866 but gives no information about where that studio was located."},{"no":6,"type":"tfng","stem":"Henderson gave up portraiture so that he could focus on taking photographs of scenery.","answer":"T","explain":"'From about 1870 he dropped portraiture to specialize in landscape photography and other views.'"},{"no":7,"type":"tfng","stem":"When Henderson began work for the Intercolonial Railway, the Montreal to Halifax line had been finished.","answer":"F","explain":"In 1875 he was commissioned to record the principal structures 'along the almost-completed line connecting Montreal to Halifax', so the line was not finished."},{"no":8,"type":"tfng","stem":"Henderson’s last work as a photographer was with the Canadian Pacific Railway.","answer":"T","explain":"He held a full-time CPR post until 1897, 'when he retired completely from photography' - the CPR was his last photographic employer."}]},{"id":"tb-u1c","unit":"u1","text":"u1c","title":"Unit 1 · Text C · Exercise 1","sourcePages":[15],"items":[{"no":1,"type":"tfng","stem":"Marie Curie’s husband was a joint winner of both Marie’s Nobel Prizes.","answer":"F","explain":"The 1903 Physics prize was shared with her husband and Becquerel, but for the 1911 Chemistry prize she was 'sole winner', so Pierre was not a joint winner of both."},{"no":2,"type":"tfng","stem":"Marie became interested in science when she was a child.","answer":"NG","explain":"The passage praises her prodigious childhood memory but never says when her interest in science began."},{"no":3,"type":"tfng","stem":"Marie was able to attend the Sorbonne because of her sister’s financial contribution.","answer":"T","explain":"Her earnings financed Bronia's medical studies 'on the understanding that Bronia would, in turn, later help her to get an education', and 'in 1891 this promise was fulfilled' when Marie went to the Sorbonne."},{"no":4,"type":"tfng","stem":"Marie stopped doing research for several years when her children were born.","answer":"F","explain":"'The births of Marie's two daughters, Irene and Eve, in 1897 and 1904 failed to interrupt her scientific work.'"},{"no":5,"type":"tfng","stem":"Marie took over the teaching position her husband had held.","answer":"T","explain":"In 1906 'she was appointed to the professorship that had been left vacant on her husband's death'."},{"no":6,"type":"tfng","stem":"Marie’s sister Bronia studied the medical uses of radioactivity.","answer":"NG","explain":"Bronia is mentioned as a medical student and later as director of the Radium Institute in Warsaw, but not as studying the medical uses of radioactivity."}]},{"id":"tb-u2b","unit":"u2","text":"u2b","title":"Unit 2 · Text B · Exercise 2","sourcePages":[24],"items":[{"no":1,"type":"tfng","stem":"Children with good self-control are known to be likely to do well at school later on.","answer":"T","explain":"'the ability to self-regulate has been shown to be a key predictor of academic performance.'"},{"no":2,"type":"tfng","stem":"The way a child plays may provide information about possible medical problems.","answer":"T","explain":"Observing children at play 'can give us important clues about their well-being and can even be useful in the diagnosis of neurodevelopmental disorders like autism.'"},{"no":3,"type":"tfng","stem":"Playing with dolls was found to benefit girls’ writing more than boy’s writing.","answer":"NG","explain":"The study found children wrote longer, better-structured stories after playing with dolls, but it makes no comparison between girls' and boys' writing."},{"no":4,"type":"tfng","stem":"Children had problems thinking up ideas when they first created the story with Lego.","answer":"F","explain":"'With the Lego building, however, not a single child said this through the whole year of the project.'"},{"no":5,"type":"tfng","stem":"People nowadays regard children’s play as less significant than they did in the past.","answer":"T","explain":"'Somehow the importance of play has been lost in recent decades, it's regarded as something trivial' - less significant than in the past."}]},{"id":"tb-u2c","unit":"u2","text":"u2c","title":"Unit 2 · Text C · Exercise 2","sourcePages":[28],"items":[{"no":1,"type":"tfng","stem":"Children need toys in order to play.","answer":"F","explain":"The drive to play is so intense that children play 'in any circumstances, for instance when they have no real toys', so toys are not needed."},{"no":2,"type":"tfng","stem":"It is a mistake to treat play and learning as separate types of activities.","answer":"T","explain":"'Our society has created a false dichotomy between play and learning.'"},{"no":3,"type":"tfng","stem":"Play helps children to develop their artistic talents.","answer":"NG","explain":"The passage lists social, cognitive, physical and emotional benefits and creative problem-solving skills, but never mentions artistic talents."},{"no":4,"type":"tfng","stem":"Researchers have agreed on a definition of play.","answer":"F","explain":"'Full consensus on a formal definition of play continues to elude the researchers and theorists who study it.'"},{"no":5,"type":"tfng","stem":"Work and play differ in terms of whether or not they have a target.","answer":"T","explain":"Play 'has no extrinsically motivated function or goal', whereas work 'is extrinsically motivated (i.e. it is goal oriented)'."}]},{"id":"tb-u3b","unit":"u3","text":"u3b","title":"Unit 3 · Text B · Exercise 1","sourcePages":[35,36],"items":[{"no":1,"type":"tfng","stem":"A shrinking organisation tends to lose its less skilled employees rather than its more skilled employees.","answer":"F","explain":"When an organisation is shrinking, 'the best and most mobile workers are prone to leave voluntarily ... those with the highest skills and experience', while the minor employees stay because their options are limited."},{"no":2,"type":"tfng","stem":"It is easier to manage a small business than a large business.","answer":"NG","explain":"The passage contrasts running a small business with a managerial post in a larger organisation, but never compares how easy the two are to manage."},{"no":3,"type":"tfng","stem":"High achievers are well suited to team work.","answer":"F","explain":"High achievers 'will do best when the job provides moderately challenging goals and where there is independence and feedback'; a bureaucratic manager should have a low need for affiliation."},{"no":4,"type":"tfng","stem":"Some employees can feel manipulated when asked to participate in goal-setting.","answer":"T","explain":"'If participation and the culture are incongruous, employees are likely to perceive the participation process as manipulative and be negatively affected by it.'"},{"no":5,"type":"tfng","stem":"The staff appraisal process should be designed by employees.","answer":"NG","explain":"The text requires employees to 'regard the appraisal process as valid' but says nothing about who should design that process."},{"no":6,"type":"tfng","stem":"Employees’ earnings should be disclosed to everyone within the organisation.","answer":"T","explain":"Rewards should be made more visible by 'eliminating the secrecy surrounding pay by openly communicating everyone's remuneration'."}]},{"id":"tb-u4b","unit":"u4","text":"u4b","title":"Unit 4 · Text B · Exercise 2","sourcePages":[49],"items":[{"no":1,"type":"tfng","stem":"In 1887, HM Ashley had the fastest bottle-producing machine that existed at the time.","answer":"T","explain":"Ashley's machine made '200 bottles per hour ... more than three times quicker than any previous production method'."},{"no":2,"type":"tfng","stem":"Michael Owens was hired by a large US company to design a fully-automated bottle manufacturing machine for them.","answer":"F","explain":"Owens was the 'founder of the Owens Bottle Machine Company', which installed his machine, so he was not hired by a large company to design it."},{"no":3,"type":"tfng","stem":"Nowadays, most glass is produced by large international manufacturers.","answer":"NG","explain":"The passage calls glass making 'big business' in 'a fiercely competitive global market' but never says most glass is made by large international manufacturers."},{"no":4,"type":"tfng","stem":"Concern for the environment is leading to an increased demand for glass containers.","answer":"T","explain":"'with growing consumer concern for green issues, glass bottles and jars are becoming ever more popular.'"},{"no":5,"type":"tfng","stem":"It is more expensive to produce recycled glass than to manufacture new glass.","answer":"F","explain":"'less energy is needed to melt recycled glass than to melt down raw materials, this also saves fuel and production costs' - recycled glass is cheaper, not more expensive."}]}]};
